{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T21:48:26.632Z","ordinal":35621,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20014-b98e-7f50-9b44-1c38f2a1201a","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-9a4497e0-6a9c-4647-bf04-61149fa80ac6","process_id":"23181","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/v1.3.0 | head -80 && git show origin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,240p' && git show origin/main:docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,80p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/v1.3.0 | head -80 && git show origin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,240p' && git show origin/main:docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md | sed -n '1,80p'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.3.0/BATCH.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md\n# Nexus Code v1.3 plan: the platform track\n\nStatus: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\n\n## 1. Purpose and theme\n\nFrontier clients are platforms: a CLI plus a desktop app, IDE integration, background and scheduled agents, an SDK or managed API, installable plugin bundles, and enterprise policy (web sources of 2026-11-15, mostly third-party; see [`docs/v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](../v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md) §2). Nexus is a single-binary Linux CLI and TUI. It cannot match breadth or first-party model fit, and a worse copy of three funded platforms is not a goal.\n\nThe v1.3 theme: **let other programs drive Nexus and let teams govern it, without weakening the gate.** Every new surface (an IDE over ACP, a scheduler, a hook, a plugin, an SDK caller, a managed policy) goes through the same approval and verification gates as the terminal does. The differentiator to build on is the one nobody else ships by default: a completion you can audit (independent verification, receipts, evidence). Two consequences shape the plan:\n\n1. **Nexus as the verification layer for other harnesses.** `nexus verify` and an MCP/ACP `verify` tool let a Codex, Claude Code or Aider session ask Nexus to independently check its work. This costs little (the machinery exists) and is positioned where the platforms are weakest.\n2. **Governance as a product.** Managed policy with fixed precedence, a hash-chained and optionally signed audit export, and hooks that can only tighten.\n\nNon-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\n\n## 2. Evidence and its limits\n\n- **Nexus today** (read-only inventory, 2026-11-15; \"present\" means present in source or docs, nothing was run): sidecar/TUI protocol is NDJSON over the sidecar's stdio with `PROTOCOL_VERSION = 1`, an event `v`, a one-way `hello`, typed unions in `cli/src/protocol.ts:53-204` hand-mirrored in `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/protocol.rs`, **no schema file, no socket**; `nexus run --json` returns `TaskExecutionResult` (`cli/src/core/app.ts:44`) with no schema or version, exit codes defined only in code (0 verified, 2 completed-unverified, 130 interrupted, 1 otherwise); `ApplicationCore` is an accidental re-export (`cli/src/index.ts:1240`) and `cli/package.json` has `main` and `bin` but no `exports` or `types`; operations are durable and resumable (per-operation lock, 12 h wait survives restart, `maxConcurrentUnits` capped at 64 and clamped to 1 without `git.isolate`) but **foreground-only**, with no scheduler, cron, trigger or daemon in the code; plugins are plain JS modules with no manifest, checksum or install command, workspace plugins load after `nexus trust` with full process authority, hooks cover pre/post tool only; policy is the workspace-local `.nexus-security.json`, **fails open to defaults on a parse error**, with no system or admin layer; session export is `nexus-session-export/2` reachable only through `/export`; the flight log is append-only by convention (no hash chain found) and the CERI evidence store hashes files but has no signature or verify command; local OTLP-style traces are written on every session with no opt-in gate and no network exporter found; a goal (objective ledger) persists across sessions via `--resume`, but nothing re-launches a task on its own.\n- **Peers, read from source** (shallow clones of 2026-11-13/14; Codex at commit 336b19a; untrusted code, nothing run): ACP servers in goose (`crates/goose/src/acp`), Cline (`apps/cli/src/acp/acpAgent.ts`), Gemini CLI (`packages/cli/src/acp`) and opencode (`packages/cli/src/acp/agent.ts`), the Codex `app-server` (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, websocket or UDS; schema generated from Rust types and checked in); schedulers in goose (`scheduler/full.rs`), Cline (`*.cron.md` and event specs) and opencode (a `ScheduleTable` with `in`/`at`/`every`/`cron` and an optional deterministic wake `check`); hooks in Gemini CLI (`docs/hooks/reference.md`), goose (`hooks/mod.rs`), Cline and Codex; extension manifests in Gemini CLI (`gemini-extension.json`), goose (Open Plugins and Gemini formats) and opencode; policy tiers in Gemini CLI (`core/src/policy/config.ts:67-71`), opencode (`managed-policy.ts`) and Codex (`requirements.toml`); goal state in opencode (`schema/src/goal.ts`) and Codex (`thread/goal/*`).\n- **XERJ reference queries** (2026-11-15, local node, read-only; the `opencode` index is flagged \"not verified complete\", so a miss there is not proof of absence): `xerj code opencode` for ACP, scheduling and plugin manifests; `xerj search --prefix goose` for ACP, scheduler and extension manifests; `xerj search --prefix xc-opencode` for managed policy. **Took:** opencode's wire-level ACP fixture pattern (`packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) for protocol conformance tests; opencode's schedule model (`schema/src/schedule.ts`, `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`: `next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome`, deterministic wake check with hard limits) and its documented GitHub scheduled-workflow example; goose's cron-on-a-recipe scheduler shape (`scheduler/full.rs`, `commands/schedule.rs`); goose's checked-in ACP schema and method metadata (`acp-meta.json`); goose's plugin formats (`plugins/formats/gemini.rs`, `open_plugins.rs`); opencode's managed policy as config statements with an action, a resource and an effect (`config/plugin/policy.ts`). **Rejected:** opencode's in-process Effect plugin hooks (a JS-in-process model is what Nexus is trying to move away from), goose's per-run `Auto` mode for scheduled runs (unattended runs must never get allow-always), and any code copied verbatim: reference code is read-only inspiration.\n- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\n- **Not covered:** Claude Code and agy were not read (web sources only); parts of Cline's daemon, Codex's hook handler types and plugin manifest, and goose's managed policy were sampled, not read in full; peer behaviour under load was not measured.\n\n## 3. Principles\n\n1. **Protocol first.** Nothing else in this plan is safe to build until the client protocol is published, schema'd, versioned and conformance-tested.\n2. **One gate.** A new surface can request an approval but can never answer one on behalf of Nexus's own gates (SI-2, CS-* rules, verification). A client that answers \"allow\" to a denied action must have no effect; this is tested.\n3. **Tighten only.** Hooks and managed policy may deny, require a human, or rewrite arguments toward safer values. They never grant.\n4. **Unattended means stricter.** Scheduled and detached runs never get allow-always, run in a fresh worktree, and record that they were unattended in every receipt.\n5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\n6. **Schemas come from types.** Generate JSON Schema from the TypeScript types and check it in with a parity test, so the Rust mirror cannot drift (reference: Codex `app-server-protocol` schema export, goose `acp-schema.json`).\n7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\n8. **Experimental is a flag.** New methods sit behind `experimental` at `initialize` (reference: Codex `experimentalApi`) until a conformance suite has run against two real clients.\n\n## 4. Workstreams and units\n\nSize: S = one PR; M = two to four PRs; L = a program. P0 = prerequisite, P1 = core, P2 = stretch.\n\n### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\n| PF-2 | `nexus run --json` result schema with a `schema_version`, documented exit codes in the manual, `--input-json` or stdin for the task | schema validated in tests; a consumer test parses the output | S | P0 |\n| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\n| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\n\n### AC: ACP and IDE access\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AC-1 | ACP adapter over the sidecar protocol (stdio), hand-rolled JSON-RPC with no dependency: `initialize` with a capability list, `session/new`, `session/load`, `session/prompt`, `session/cancel`; streaming `tool_call` and `tool_call_update` with diff content; a `verification` update carrying receipt ids and verifier results; client-bound `session/request_permission` with allow-once and reject only (no \"allow always\" at first); one extension, `_nexus/receipt/get`. Reference: goose, Cline, opencode, Gemini CLI ACP servers and the Codex app-server (all four ACP servers use the same three-option permission request; opencode attaches the diff to the request so the user decides with the change in view). The host side exists too: OpenHands' Agent Canvas drives Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode over ACP (docs, 2026-11-15), so an ACP server is what makes Nexus drivable by such hosts | a scripted ACP client completes a task; an `allow` reply to an action the gates deny has no effect (gate test G-3); the receipt is retrievable | L | P1 |\n| AC-2 | ACP conformance: fixture suite plus runs against two real ACP clients (decision D14), recorded as fixtures; no live model | both clients complete the basic flow; fixtures committed | M | P1 |\n\nThis supersedes v1.2.1 unit RD-5 (ACP server mode), which moves here.\n\n### VR: Nexus as the verification layer\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| VR-1 | `nexus verify` (no agent run): given a worktree or a base and head, run the discovered or configured verifier, the type-check evidence (CG-4), the verification-file guard and the independent reviewer, and print a verdict VERIFIED / REGRESSION_FREE / UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED with the receipt; exit code per the documented contract; usable as a CI gate (pairs with the v1.2.1 GitHub Action, RD-4) | a known-bad diff (weakened test) is not VERIFIED; the same diff produced by another harness gives the same verdict | M | P1 |\n| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\n\n### BG: background and scheduled runs\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BG-1 | Detached runner: a user-level job runner that owns durable operations so a run survives its CLI process (decision D15: a documented `systemd --user` unit versus a self-daemonizing mode); foreground mode unchanged | kill the CLI, the run continues and resumes cleanly; the SI-7 kill test passes against the runner | L | P1 |\n| BG-2 | Schedules: a spec (trigger `cron`, `every` or `once`; objective ledger id; `max_rounds`; timeout; policy `read-only` or `worktree-write`; optional deterministic wake `check` command with hard limits, reference opencode `schedule/check.ts:23-39`: 30 s, 20 tool calls, 32 KB, cannot create schedules or worktrees) stored beside operations (`next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome` as in opencode `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`); `nexus schedule add\\|list\\|rm\\|run-now`; each run in a fresh worktree; unattended flag in every receipt; no allow-always; delivery is a receipt plus a pointer file | a scheduled run on a seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED and says so; an unattended run asking for approval is denied, not blocked forever; missed runs are reported, not silently skipped | L | P1 |\n| BG-3 | Event triggers (file change, webhook) | deferred: needs a trust and abuse model first | M | P2 (v1.4) |\n\n### GV: governance\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GV-1 | Managed policy with fixed precedence **system > user > project**: `/etc/nexus/policy.toml` (path per decision D16), `~/.config/nexus/policy.toml`, then the existing `.nexus-security.json`; restrict-only semantics (deny, require a human, disable auto/yolo, allowlist MCP servers and plugins, set verification-path modes); the effective tier is printed beside every decision; a parse error in the system or user tier **fails closed**; a project policy file is hashed and a changed one asks again (reference: Gemini CLI tiers, MATCH/MISMATCH/NEW integrity check and system settings paths; opencode managed policy; Codex `requirements.toml`) | a project policy cannot loosen a system policy; a corrupted system policy refuses to start with a clear message instead of failing open; precedence table covered by tests | M | P1 |\n| GV-2 | Audit export: `nexus audit export <task\\|session>` writes a bundle (session export, receipts, evidence, verification attempts, flight log excerpt) with a manifest of SHA-256 hashes and a hash chain, optionally signed with an operator-provided ed25519 key (`node:crypto`, decision D17); `nexus audit verify` | a bundle with one altered byte fails verification and names the file; an unsigned bundle verifies by hash only and says so | M | P1 |\n| GV-3 | Hash-chain the flight recorder and the verification-attempt log (today append-only by convention only) and verify the chain on `nexus flights replay` | truncating or editing a record is detected | S | P1 |\n| GV-4 | Telemetry transparency (this also settles v1.2.1 unit PC-5): document what the local traces contain, add a setting to disable them and a retention limit, state plainly that nothing leaves the host (no network exporter found) | doc plus a test that no network call is made | S | P1 |\n| GV-5 | Admin controls from the system policy: a strict mode that disables auto and full-access, an MCP and plugin allowlist | strict mode refuses `--yes` and `--mode auto` with the policy source named | S | P1 |\n\n### HK: hooks and plugins\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HK-1 | Declarative command hooks: events `pre-tool`, `post-tool`, `pre-verify`, `post-verify`, `permission-request`, `session-start`, `session-end`, `stop`, `pre-compact`; JSON on stdin (`session_id`, `cwd`, `hook_event_name`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`) and on stdout (`decision`, `reason`, `hookSpecificOutput`), exit 2 blocks with stderr as the reason, a timeout, a scrubbed environment (reuse `buildMcpEnvironment`), results written to the receipt. A hook may only deny or tighten arguments, never grant (Gemini CLI also ignores `allow` from lower tiers). Project-defined hooks need a trust decision keyed by a hash of the command (reference: Gemini CLI `trusted_hooks.json`, goose `hooks/mod.rs`, Codex hook trust). Handler types: command only (no peer shipped http, prompt or subagent handlers in what was read) | a hook that returns `allow` for a denied action has no effect; a changed project hook asks again; the contract is documented so existing hook scripts of that shape transfer | M | P1 |\n| HK-2 | Plugin manifest `nexus-plugin.json` (name, version, declared contributions: hooks, skills, verifiers, MCP servers; required permissions); install from a local path or a git URL **pinned to a commit hash**; capabilities shown at install, resolved commit recorded in an install receipt; `nexus plugins install\\|list\\|remove\\|verify`; no marketplace and no auto-update (reference: Gemini CLI `gemini-extension.json`, goose `.goose-plugin-install.json`). Skills load on a declared trigger and are not always-on (references: OpenHands trigger-based skills, the on-demand-loading guidance of `agent-rules-books`). Also fix the documented example in `docs/SKILLS_AND_PLUGINS.md` that does not match the code (`onTurnStart` positional arguments versus a context object) and stop swallowing plugin load errors silently | installing a plugin whose declared permissions exceed the system policy is refused; `verify` detects a modified install; a skill whose trigger did not fire adds nothing to the request (request-shape snapshot) | M | P1 |\n| HK-3 | Per-plugin trust instead of per-directory, and run workspace-defined plugin hooks as subprocesses rather than in-process (a trusted workspace today runs every `.js` file in it with full process authority) | decision D18; migration note for existing plugins | M | P2 |\n\n### PA: parallel agents and goals\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\n| PA-2 | Verified merge arbitration: when parallel units integrate, apply each diff, run verification on the merged tree, reject on failure. No peer read does more than isolate; this is where Nexus can differ | two units whose changes conflict semantically (each passes alone) fail verification on the merged tree and neither is integrated silently | M | P1 |\n| PA-3 | Goal semantics: a ledger entry active from an earlier process resumes **paused** (opencode `goal.ts` boot id), stall counting by failure fingerprint and not turn count, completion decided only by a verifier receipt, an optional token budget rolling up from sub-operations (reference: Codex goal budget); `/team` leaves experimental only after PA-1 and PA-2 | stale goal resumes paused with a visible reason; a model \"I am done\" does not complete a goal | M | P2 |\n| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\n\n## 5. Sequencing\n\nPhase A (foundation, parallelisable): PF-1, PF-2, GV-4, VR-1, GV-3. These unblock the rest and cost little.\nPhase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2.\nPhase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5.\nPhase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\nPA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\n\nDependencies on earlier plans: v1.2.1 RD-3 (`stream-json` output) feeds PF-2; RD-4 (GitHub Action) pairs with VR-1; CS-9 (per-command approval rules, v1.2.2) and GV-1 must agree on rule semantics (allow < prompt < forbidden, deciding rule recorded); MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split) makes AC-1 and BG-1 much easier and should land first if possible; PR-* (provider registry) is independent; MQ-* (measurement) should publish at least one result before this release claims anything.\n\n## 6. Release gates\n\n| Gate | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| G-1 | `make test` green at the exact head; required CI green; main CI green on the merge commit |\n| G-2 | Protocol conformance: schema parity test and the wire-fixture suite green; AC-1 passes against two real clients or the release says \"experimental\" |\n| G-3 | **Gate-bypass suite:** a hostile client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy file cannot approve a denied action, raise a verdict, widen a policy tier, or remove a verification-file protection |\n| G-4 | Unattended runs: tests show no allow-always, a fresh worktree, the unattended flag in receipts, and a kill-and-resume pass |\n| G-5 | Audit round trip: export, verify, tamper one byte, verify fails and names the file |\n| G-6 | Docs and examples for every new surface (`docs/PROTOCOL.md`, hooks, plugins, policy, schedules, audit) with runnable examples in CI |\n| G-7 | Notes keep the framing: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" unless an external result (E2 per the v1.2.1 plan §7) exists |\n\n## 7. Decisions needed\n\n| Id | Decision | Default if unanswered |\n|---|---|---|\n| D13 | Publish an npm package or SDK (PF-4)? Package name and licence; external publishing is yours to authorise | no publish; the entry point is documented but not published |\n| D14 | Which two ACP clients to test against (AC-2) | the two most widely used open-source ones that run on Linux without an account |\n| D15 | Detached runner model: a documented `systemd --user` unit, or a self-daemonizing mode (BG-1) | the `systemd --user` unit only |\n| D16 | System policy location and whether the system and user tiers fail closed on a parse error (GV-1) | `/etc/nexus/policy.toml`; fail closed |\n| D17 | Audit signing: where the operator's ed25519 key lives and whether signing is required or optional (GV-2) | optional; key path supplied by the operator, never stored by Nexus |\n| D18 | Whether in-process workspace JS plugins stay supported after HK-1/HK-2 (HK-3) | keep, but move to per-plugin trust with a deprecation note |\n| D19 | Unattended-run defaults (BG-2): `read-only` only, or `worktree-write` allowed | `read-only` by default; `worktree-write` per schedule with an explicit flag |\n| D20 | Position Nexus as a verifier for other harnesses (VR-2)? | yes |\n| D21 | Event triggers and remote (SSH) execution: v1.4 or never | v1.4 candidate, no work in v1.3 |\n\n## 8. Risks\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| A new socket or listener widens the attack surface | unix socket mode 0600 by default; token and origin check for any HTTP listener; G-3 |\n| A client answers approvals in a way that bypasses a gate | the gate decides first; the client only supplies a human's yes or no; G-3 tests it |\n| Fail-closed policy locks an operator out | a clear message naming the file and tier, and a documented recovery path; tested |\n| Unattended runs burn the shared gateway or contend with live measurements | schedules use the `GATEWAY` claim protocol; read-only default; run budget in every spec |\n| Plugin supply chain | commit-pinned installs, capabilities shown, no auto-update, install receipt, `verify` |\n| Protocol churn after publication | `experimental` flag, version range in `hello`, schema diff in CI |\n| Scope: this track is as large as v1.2.0 plus v1.2.1 | the sequencing above; Phase D rolls without ceremony; a unit is never half-merged |\n| An unreviewed peer assumption | sources and limits are listed in section 2; each unit's PR repeats the reference coding record (XERJ queries, what was taken or rejected) |\n# Nexus Code v1.2.1 plan\n\nStatus: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes. Evidence and the full gap register are in [`GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](GAP_ANALYSIS.md); gap ids (GA-nn) refer to it. Owner decisions are recorded in section 6 (answered 2026-11-15) and nothing marked \"decision\" starts without one.\n\n## 1. Purpose and theme\n\nv1.2.0 made \"done\" mean something (independent verification, typed requirements, guarded verification files, convergence stopping, receipts with real dependencies) and said plainly that none of it is independent qualification. v1.2.1 is about **measuring it, shipping it to more people, containing it by default, and hardening what shipped**:\n\n1. **Measure** the core claim (fewer false completions) on public benchmarks and on real projects.\n2. **Reach**: platforms and integration points peers already have.\n3. **Contain** by default, and close the record-only gaps.\n4. **Harden** weak-model handling and context.\n5. **Pay down** the process and maintainability debt that made v1.2.0 take a week of CI.\n\nNon-goals: new verification features beyond the deferred SI-5/DV-3/DV-4 set, new providers, a hosted product, any claim of frontier-grade.\n\n## 2. Rules for this release\n\n- Every unit is test-first, one PR, with local Code / Security / Slop reviews on the exact head before merge (prompts: `memory/entire-agent-review-prompts.md`), required CI green at the exact head, and honest framing in notes: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" until an external result exists.\n- No new runtime dependency without an owner decision. No change to tags (immutable). Dependabot PRs roll in.\n- A behaviour change that can break an existing user (containment default, concurrency validation, shell-write hard stop) is a decision (section 6), a release-notes line, and an opt-out.\n- A unit that finds the plan wrong says so in its PR and updates this file.\n- Live inference is the project's gateway only; any live measurement holds the `GATEWAY` claim and uses the key from the environment, never printed.\n\n## 3. Release gates\n\nv1.2.1 ships when:\n\n| Gate | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| G-1 | `make test` green at the exact head; required CI green on the release commit; main CI green on the merge commit |\n| G-2 | Every P0 unit merged; every P1 unit merged or carries a dated owner deferral |\n| G-3 | Live-gateway tool-call matrix (G1.1-4) repeated on the release-candidate archive and recorded as evidence (also closes GA-03 for v1.2.0 if still open) |\n| G-4 | A first external-style measurement exists and is published in the notes with its limits (MQ-2): at minimum a pinned Terminal-Bench subset run through the Harbor adapter, with the false-completion rate reported beside the pass rate. If no run is possible in the window, the notes say \"no external measurement\" and the release is still allowed; the framing never changes |\n| G-5 | No test in the suite can leak a bridge, agent, gateway, child process or timer past its file (PC-2 guard green) |\n| G-6 | Docs: README, CHANGELOG, manual and examples current (DH-1…DH-3) |\n\n\"Externally qualified\" is defined in section 7 so the word has one meaning.\n\n## 4. Workstreams and units\n\nSize: S = one PR; M = two to four PRs; L = a program. P0 = must-have, P1 = should-have, P2 = stretch (may roll to v1.2.2).\n\n### MQ: measure and qualify\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MQ-0 | Run the G1.1-4 matrix on the v1.2.0 release-candidate archive under the GATEWAY claim; record `docs/evidence/v1.2.0/…` and add the figure to the rc notes (closes GA-03) | evidence file with archive sha256, per-alias counts, repaired / invalid counts; notes updated by a docs PR | S | P0 |\n| MQ-1 | Benchmark adapter: a Harbor / Terminal-Bench runner for Nexus (template: Goose `evals/harbor/`), plus a SWE-bench-style adapter if the task format allows a hermetic run | `nexus` runs a pinned subset in task containers with no network leaks; results JSON with task ids, model, gateway alias, Nexus version, seed or sampling, wall time, and the raw verdicts (VERIFIED / REGRESSION_FREE / UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED) | M | P0 |\n| MQ-2 | False-completion metric: for every run record Nexus's own verdict and the benchmark's ground truth; report pass rate, **claimed-done-but-wrong rate**, and **verified-but-wrong rate** with Wilson 95% intervals (the repo already has the interval code in `eval/score.mjs`); compare against the same model through at least one peer harness already adapted in `eval/configs/pilot-*.json` | a report where a false VERIFIED is named and explained; the notes state the sample size and that it is self-run | M | P0 |\n| MQ-3 | Real-project smoke: a nightly job that runs a scripted task on pinned copies of representative repositories (a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project, a Python project, a Rust project, and the owner's own repositories where licensing allows) and fails when `task_completed` errors, hangs or ends UNVERIFIED for an avoidable reason. This is the test that would have caught the v1.1.4 every-completion error before release | job red on a seeded regression (a repo whose verifier cannot be bound must complete as REGRESSION_FREE). **Done 2026-11-17** (branch `v1/mq3`): `scripts/real_project_smoke.mjs`, `cli/test/real_project_smoke.test.ts`, nightly `.github/workflows/real-project-smoke.yml` (not required), `docs/REAL_PROJECT_SMOKE.md`. Covers a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project with 140 referenced files, a 24-deep tree and a pytest project; red when a completion errors, hangs or is not REGRESSION_FREE/VERIFIED; mutation-checked against a reintroduced unbindable-verifier block. **Does not cover:** a live model (the gateway is a scripted mock), real third-party or owner repositories (all synthetic), a Rust project, sandbox, worktree or resume modes | M | P0 **First hosted run 2026-11-18 (schedule, main 3c54e601): 3 of 4 repositories passed, the pytest one failed on a setup defect (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible to the isolated HOME); fixed, rerun to be recorded.** **Validated on a hosted runner 2026-11-18 (manual run 44314195365 on a7c07e74: all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE) after the pytest setup fix.** |\n| MQ-4 | Scheduled model conformance: a nightly or weekly job that runs the tool-call matrix against the configured gateway aliases and alerts on a drop; claim protocol so it never collides with a human run (template: Goose `model-toolcall-conformance.yml`) | one alert path tested with a forced failure | S | P1 |\n| MQ-5 | CERI baseline identity bound to evidence (audit F02): baseline manifest, model digest, sampling and configuration recorded and verified when a comparison is made; a comparison without bound baselines is labelled non-certifying (decision D7, included) | a comparison against an edited baseline fails; test with a swapped manifest | M | P0 |\n| MQ-6 | CERI evidence loading verifies the integrity record (audit F06): `passed:\"false\"` no longer loads as true, run-id collisions are refused, a tampered record is rejected (D7) | tests for each of the three defects | M | P0 |\n| MQ-7 | Audit Slices B–G, decide each as a unit or a dated deferral, then build in this order: B evidence lifecycle, C verified baselines and paired comparisons (needed for MQ-2 to be called certifying), E request-context manifests, D installed-evaluator adapter, F recovery reference checker, G bounded campaigns (D7). B and C are P1; D, E, F, G are P2 and may roll | each slice either merged with tests or carries a dated deferral in this file. **2026-11-17:** slices B and C built (branch `v1/mq7bc`; builds on MQ-5/MQ-6): B adds resolved (realpath) containment of evidence paths, validated run ids (reserved names, length, case-only collisions) and the written same-user limitation in `docs/CERI_EVIDENCE.md`; C adds a fixture identity, `mismatchedFields` naming every differing identity field, `loadVerifiedBaseline`/`compareToVerifiedBaseline`, and removes the caller flag as a route to CONFIRMED_REGRESSION (a verified earlier run is required). **Deferred 2026-11-17 (D7):** slices D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker) and G (bounded campaigns) roll to the next release (v1.2.2) per D7; not started | L | P1/P2 |\n| MQ-8 | Long-horizon and A/B measurement (the Anthropic trends report, trend 3; `agent-rules-books` and the context-management post both report gains without a methodology): one multi-hour scripted task on a pinned repository with a resume part-way, and paired runs of the same scenarios with an instruction file, or tool-output clearing, on and off; report completion, claimed-done-but-wrong rate, tokens and wall time with Wilson 95% intervals | results JSON with the paired runs, model, gateway alias, Nexus version and seeds; no effect claimed unless the interval excludes zero (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2) | M | P2 (v1.2.2) |\n\nDependencies: MQ-1 → MQ-2 → MQ-8. MQ-3 and MQ-4 are independent. Decision D3 (what is published) and D4 (scope and cost) gate MQ-1 and MQ-2.\n\n### VC: verification completeness (the v1.2.0 deferrals)\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| VC-1 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/core/boundary_rules.ts`, operation spec `boundaries`, enforced in the review gate; limits in docs/USER_MANUAL.md). SI-5 operator-declared boundary rules (import allow/deny, protected public files) checked on the candidate diff with deterministic analysis; interface/schema/format changes classified compatible / potentially breaking / unknown; coverage limits always reported | boundary violation = REJECT; undeclared dependency = advisory; unknown coverage disclosed; rules absent = no behaviour change; CERI `cumulative-change-03` becomes active | L | P1 |\n| VC-2 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/core/regression_obligations.ts`; operator verbs on `/requirements`; `task_completed` is refused while `unmetObligations` is non-empty, naming each obligation and its replay command, and a recorded pass of that command lets it proceed; weakening is recorded for file-tool edits, including headless runs with no UI observer, and for `run_command` edits via the CS-2 shell snapshot. Limits: a shell edit is seen only for files of at most 64 KiB inside the scan bounds; replay is recorded when the verifier runs, and a verifier that never runs the test passes it vacuously). DV-3 operator-authorised regression obligations: a confirmed failing test becomes a protected obligation only on operator authorisation; the original failure record is kept when later minimised | authorised obligation replayed on the next candidate and a weakening edit recorded; unauthorised promotion cannot happen | M | P2 |\n| VC-3 | **Done 2026-11-18.** Lineage (parent attempt, transition hash of the two candidate identities: not a content diff), the authorisation gate for a named reference (`/requirements reference`) and the behavioural differential (`/requirements differential <R> --inputs <file>`) are built. The reference is an operator-declared command; the candidate is the objective's latest verifier command; each declared input (JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) goes to both on stdin through the sandbox with no network, 30 s per run, output capped at 1 MiB; outputs are normalised with `normalizeOutcomeText` and compared with the exit code. A timeout, error or over-cap run is inconclusive for that input, never agreement. The result is finite agreement on the tested inputs (`agreementStatement`), never equivalence; there is no model-facing path. DV-4 attempt lineage (parent attempt, diff hash) and behavioural differential against a declared reference only when an authorised requirement names one (the Anthropic 2026 trends report's flagship long run, Rakuten's seven-hour single run at 99.9% numerical accuracy against a reference method, succeeded because a reference existed: the reference is what lets a long run stay autonomous. Raising this to P1 if MQ-1 shows reference-checkable tasks are common is an owner decision, not made here) | baseline-defect correction not blocked when an authoritative requirement contradicts the baseline; finite-case agreement never reported as equivalence | M | P2 |\n| VC-4 | **Done 2026-11-17:** the only pending scenario, `cumulative-change-03`, is active (pending count 0); `compaction-03` became active with CX-5. Close the two pending CERI scenarios once VC-1 lands; assign an owner to every scenario in `semantic_integrity` | pending count 0 or each pending carries a unit id | S | P1 |\n| VC-5 | Re-check `verified` on resume (invalidate when the candidate identity differs) and persist the unbound-pass marker so a resumed task does not hit the \"no matching baseline\" refusal | resume test: edited workspace demotes `verified`; resume after an unbound pass completes as REGRESSION_FREE | S | P1 |\n| VC-6 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/tester.ts`, `cli/src/core/completion_oracle.ts`, `cli/src/git.ts`, `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts`; manual section \"Type-check evidence\"). Baseline run (tsc, go vet, pyright, mypy; cargo excluded as not cheap) so an untouched caller broken by the change blocks VERIFIED; shell-changed files counted via the workspace diff; Go and Python (when configured); missing tool is UNAVAILABLE, never a pass; NEXUS_TYPECHECK=off unchanged. Limits: one check per project (first found: tsc, cargo, go, then python), no baseline after a resume, a baseline needs the verifier baseline to have run. Type-check gaps: baseline run to catch callers broken in untouched files, count shell-changed files as modified (workspace diff, not edit tools), Go (`go vet`) and Python (pyright/mypy when configured) | a change that breaks an untouched caller blocks VERIFIED; opt-out stays | M | P2 |\n| VC-7 | Convergence on the `run_tests` path and for operator-configured verifiers | a `run_tests`-only loop stops with the history attached | S | P2 **Done 2026-11-18** (`dispatch_tool_call.ts` judges `convergenceSignal` after a recorded verifier attempt; `cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`, the main case was red before the fix). An operator-configured verifier is covered because it is recorded through the same path. |\n| VC-8 | Per-role tool allowlists beyond reviewer and researcher (old backlog REQ:259, accepted 2026-11-15): allowlists for implementer and tester, consistent with SI-2's tester scoping (`roleToolAllowList`, `cli/src/agent.ts:5490`) | each role's disallowed tool is refused with a reason; existing flows unchanged | S | P2 (v1.2.2) |\n| VC-9 | **Partly done 2026-11-18** (audit: `docs/v1.2.1/VC-9-needs-input-audit.md`; glue: `cli/src/core/needs_input.ts`, `--answer`). Done: tool approval (write, exec, egress), verification-file edit, verifier and type-check approval end BLOCKED / NEEDS_INPUT with a question and resume with `--answer`. **Not done** (design decisions, see the audit): a requirement marked blocked (changes the REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE contract; free-text answer), an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar-only; resolve-after-the-fact), a subagent's question. Headless needs-input audit (Anthropic 2026 trends report, trend 4: agents that learn when to ask for help): list every path where a headless run needs an operator decision (steering that negates a requirement, a requirement marked blocked, a denied approval, a verification-file edit) and give each the same resumable outcome, a stable `needs_input` reason carrying the question and resumable with the answer; build only the missing glue | each path ends BLOCKED with a machine-readable question and resumes with an answer; no path guesses | S | P2 |\n| VC-10 | Steering semantic judge (optional; evidence in `docs/research/steering-judgment/`): a judge provider interface (none by default, the configured gateway, or an explicit opt-in third party) asked, for each live requirement, whether the steering message withdraws, defers or changes it; conflict (the existing operator decision) if the regex hits or `withdraws` >= 0.7 whatever `defers` says, advisory note between 0.3 and 0.7, `defers` and `changes` informational only; an unavailable judge records `semanticJudge: unavailable` and keeps the regex decision; the steer stays held from the model until the operator decides and no text goes to a third party unless opted in | **gated**: build only after a larger evaluation (at least 100 judgments from real steering messages, with the owner's consent, labelled blind, on a fresh held-out split) shows union recall of at least 0.8 with at most 5% false positives on the non-withdrawals, and after the synchronous path can hold a steer while a judge answers (the pilot gateway judge took a median of about 17 s per message) | M | P2 (v1.2.2, gated) |\n\n### CS: containment and security\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CS-1 | Containment by default: use the existing bubblewrap path by default when available (fall back to review mode with a visible notice when not); keep `--sandbox none` | decision D1; default flips only with an opt-out and a release-notes line; doctor reports the effective containment | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17 (D1: default stays opt-in):** doctor reports the effective containment and how to enable it (`cli/src/doctor.ts`). |\n| CS-2 | Hard stop for shell writes to verification files: after a `run_command`, compare the verification-file set against the pre-command snapshot and refuse completion (or revert, per decision D2) when it changed | a `sed -i Makefile` run ends BLOCKED with the diff; ordinary commands unaffected | M | P1 |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":43676}},"started_at_ms":1795297706631,"completed_at_ms":1795297706632}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T21:48:26.799Z","ordinal":35622,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a20015-032f-72c3-a21b-2ef28b552f5f","call_id":"call_185713b96a30427d816227be1d5ffe62","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 10819)\nTotal output lines: 231\n\ndocs/v1.3.0/BATCH.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md\n# Nexus Code v1.3 plan: the platform track\n\nStatus: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\n\n## 1. Purpose and theme\n\nFrontier clients are platforms: a CLI plus a desktop app, IDE integration, background and scheduled agents, an SDK or managed API, installable plugin bundles, and enterprise policy (web sources of 2026-11-15, mostly third-party; see [`docs/v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](../v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md) §2). Nexus is a single-binary Linux CLI and TUI. It cannot match breadth or first-party model fit, and a worse copy of three funded platforms is not a goal.\n\nThe v1.3 theme: **let other programs drive Nexus and let teams govern it, without weakening the gate.** Every new surface (an IDE over ACP, a scheduler, a hook, a plugin, an SDK caller, a managed policy) goes through the same approval and verification gates as the terminal does. The differentiator to build on is the one nobody else ships by default: a completion you can audit (independent verification, receipts, evidence). Two consequences shape the plan:\n\n1. **Nexus as the verification layer for other harnesses.** `nexus verify` and an MCP/ACP `verify` tool let a Codex, Claude Code or Aider session ask Nexus to independently check its work. This costs little (the machinery exists) and is positioned where the platforms are weakest.\n2. **Governance as a product.** Managed policy with fixed precedence, a hash-chained and optionally signed audit export, and hooks that can only tighten.\n\nNon-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\n\n## 2. Evidence and its limits\n\n- **Nexus today** (read-only inventory, 2026-11-15; \"present\" means present in source or docs, nothing was run): sidecar/TUI protocol is NDJSON over the sidecar's stdio with `PROTOCOL_VERSION = 1`, an event `v`, a one-way `hello`, typed unions in `cli/src/protocol.ts:53-204` hand-mirrored in `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/protocol.rs`, **no schema file, no socket**; `nexus run --json` returns `TaskExecutionResult` (`cli/src/core/app.ts:44`) with no schema or version, exit codes defined only in code (0 verified, 2 completed-unverified, 130 interrupted, 1 otherwise); `ApplicationCore` is an accidental re-export (`cli/src/index.ts:1240`) and `cli/package.json` has `main` and `bin` but no `exports` or `types`; operations are durable and resumable (per-operation lock, 12 h wait survives restart, `maxConcurrentUnits` capped at 64 and clamped to 1 without `git.isolate`) but **foreground-only**, with no scheduler, cron, trigger or daemon in the code; plugins are plain JS modules with no manifest, checksum or install command, workspace plugins load after `nexus trust` with full process authority, hooks cover pre/post tool only; policy is the workspace-local `.nexus-security.json`, **fails open to defaults on a parse error**, with no system or admin layer; session export is `nexus-session-export/2` reachable only through `/export`; the flight log is append-only by convention (no hash chain found) and the CERI evidence store hashes files but has no signature or verify command; local OTLP-style traces are written on every session with no opt-in gate and no network exporter found; a goal (objective ledger) persists across sessions via `--resume`, but nothing re-launches a task on its own.\n- **Peers, read from source** (shallow clones of 2026-11-13/14; Codex at commit 336b19a; untrusted code, nothing run): ACP servers in goose (`crates/goose/src/acp`), Cline (`apps/cli/src/acp/acpAgent.ts`), Gemini CLI (`packages/cli/src/acp`) and opencode (`packages/cli/src/acp/agent.ts`), the Codex `app-server` (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, websocket or UDS; schema generated from Rust types and checked in); schedulers in goose (`scheduler/full.rs`), Cline (`*.cron.md` and event specs) and opencode (a `ScheduleTable` with `in`/`at`/`every`/`cron` and an optional deterministic wake `check`); hooks in Gemini CLI (`docs/hooks/reference.md`), goose (`hooks/mod.rs`), Cline and Codex; extension manifests in Gemini CLI (`gemini-extension.json`), goose (Open Plugins and Gemini formats) and opencode; policy tiers in Gemini CLI (`core/src/policy/config.ts:67-71`), opencode (`managed-policy.ts`) and Codex (`requirements.toml`); goal state in opencode (`schema/src/goal.ts`) and Codex (`thread/goal/*`).\n- **XERJ reference queries** (2026-11-15, local node, read-only; the `opencode` index is flagged \"not verified complete\", so a miss there is not proof of absence): `xerj code opencode` for ACP, scheduling and plugin manifests; `xerj search --prefix goose` for ACP, scheduler and extension manifests; `xerj search --prefix xc-opencode` for managed policy. **Took:** opencode's wire-level ACP fixture pattern (`packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) for protocol conformance tests; opencode's schedule model (`schema/src/schedule.ts`, `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`: `next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome`, deterministic wake check with hard limits) and its documented GitHub scheduled-workflow example; goose's cron-on-a-recipe scheduler shape (`scheduler/full.rs`, `commands/schedule.rs`); goose's checked-in ACP schema and method metadata (`acp-meta.json`); goose's plugin formats (`plugins/formats/gemini.rs`, `open_plugins.rs`); opencode's managed policy as config statements with an action, a resource and an effect (`config/plugin/policy.ts`). **Rejected:** opencode's in-process Effect plugin hooks (a JS-in-process model is what Nexus is trying to move away from), goose's per-run `Auto` mode for scheduled runs (unattended runs must never get allow-always), and any code copied verbatim: reference code is read-only inspiration.\n- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\n- **Not covered:** Claude Code and agy were not read (web sources only); parts of Cline's daemon, Codex's hook handler types and plugin manifest, and goose's managed policy were sampled, not read in full; peer behaviour under load was not measured.\n\n## 3. Principles\n\n1. **Protocol first.** Nothing else in this plan is safe to build until the client protocol is published, schema'd, versioned and conformance-tested.\n2. **One gate.** A new surface can request an approval but can never answer one on behalf of Nexus's own gates (SI-2, CS-* rules, verification). A client that answers \"allow\" to a denied action must have no effect; this is tested.\n3. **Tighten only.** Hooks and managed policy may deny, require a human, or rewrite arguments toward safer values. They never grant.\n4. **Unattended means stricter.** Scheduled and detached runs never get allow-always, run in a fresh worktree, and record that they were unattended in every receipt.\n5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\n6. **Schemas come from types.** Generate JSON Schema from the TypeScript types and check it in with a parity test, so the Rust mirror cannot drift (reference: Codex `app-server-protocol` schema export, goose `acp-schema.json`).\n7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\n8. **Experimental is a flag.** New methods sit behind `experimental` at `initialize` (reference: Codex `experimentalApi`) until a conformance suite has run against two real clients.\n\n## 4. Workstreams and units\n\nSize: S = one PR; M = two to four PRs; L = a program. P0 = prerequisite, P1 = core, P2 = stretch.\n\n### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\n| PF-2 | `nexus run --json` result schema with a `schema_version`, documented exit codes in the manual, `--input-json` or stdin for the task | schema validated in tests; a consumer test parses the output | S | P0 |\n| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\n| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\n\n### AC: ACP and IDE access\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AC-1 | ACP adapter over the sidecar protocol (stdio), hand-rolled JSON-RPC with no dependency: `initialize` with a capability list, `session/new`, `session/load`, `session/prompt`, `session/cancel`; streaming `tool_call` and `tool_call_update` with diff content; a `verification` update carrying receipt ids and verifier results; client-bound `session/request_permission` with allow-once and reject only (no \"allow always\" at first); one extension, `_nexus/receipt/get`. Reference: goose, Cline, opencode, Gemini CLI ACP servers and the Codex app-server (all four ACP servers use the same three-option permission request; opencode attaches the diff to the request so the user decides with the change in view). The host side exists too: OpenHands' Agent Canvas drives Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode over ACP (docs, 2026-11-15), so an ACP server is what makes Nexus drivable by such hosts | a scripted ACP client completes a task; an `all…5319 tokens truncated…e manifest, model digest, sampling and configuration recorded and verified when a comparison is made; a comparison without bound baselines is labelled non-certifying (decision D7, included) | a comparison against an edited baseline fails; test with a swapped manifest | M | P0 |\n| MQ-6 | CERI evidence loading verifies the integrity record (audit F06): `passed:\"false\"` no longer loads as true, run-id collisions are refused, a tampered record is rejected (D7) | tests for each of the three defects | M | P0 |\n| MQ-7 | Audit Slices B–G, decide each as a unit or a dated deferral, then build in this order: B evidence lifecycle, C verified baselines and paired comparisons (needed for MQ-2 to be called certifying), E request-context manifests, D installed-evaluator adapter, F recovery reference checker, G bounded campaigns (D7). B and C are P1; D, E, F, G are P2 and may roll | each slice either merged with tests or carries a dated deferral in this file. **2026-11-17:** slices B and C built (branch `v1/mq7bc`; builds on MQ-5/MQ-6): B adds resolved (realpath) containment of evidence paths, validated run ids (reserved names, length, case-only collisions) and the written same-user limitation in `docs/CERI_EVIDENCE.md`; C adds a fixture identity, `mismatchedFields` naming every differing identity field, `loadVerifiedBaseline`/`compareToVerifiedBaseline`, and removes the caller flag as a route to CONFIRMED_REGRESSION (a verified earlier run is required). **Deferred 2026-11-17 (D7):** slices D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker) and G (bounded campaigns) roll to the next release (v1.2.2) per D7; not started | L | P1/P2 |\n| MQ-8 | Long-horizon and A/B measurement (the Anthropic trends report, trend 3; `agent-rules-books` and the context-management post both report gains without a methodology): one multi-hour scripted task on a pinned repository with a resume part-way, and paired runs of the same scenarios with an instruction file, or tool-output clearing, on and off; report completion, claimed-done-but-wrong rate, tokens and wall time with Wilson 95% intervals | results JSON with the paired runs, model, gateway alias, Nexus version and seeds; no effect claimed unless the interval excludes zero (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2) | M | P2 (v1.2.2) |\n\nDependencies: MQ-1 → MQ-2 → MQ-8. MQ-3 and MQ-4 are independent. Decision D3 (what is published) and D4 (scope and cost) gate MQ-1 and MQ-2.\n\n### VC: verification completeness (the v1.2.0 deferrals)\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| VC-1 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/core/boundary_rules.ts`, operation spec `boundaries`, enforced in the review gate; limits in docs/USER_MANUAL.md). SI-5 operator-declared boundary rules (import allow/deny, protected public files) checked on the candidate diff with deterministic analysis; interface/schema/format changes classified compatible / potentially breaking / unknown; coverage limits always reported | boundary violation = REJECT; undeclared dependency = advisory; unknown coverage disclosed; rules absent = no behaviour change; CERI `cumulative-change-03` becomes active | L | P1 |\n| VC-2 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/core/regression_obligations.ts`; operator verbs on `/requirements`; `task_completed` is refused while `unmetObligations` is non-empty, naming each obligation and its replay command, and a recorded pass of that command lets it proceed; weakening is recorded for file-tool edits, including headless runs with no UI observer, and for `run_command` edits via the CS-2 shell snapshot. Limits: a shell edit is seen only for files of at most 64 KiB inside the scan bounds; replay is recorded when the verifier runs, and a verifier that never runs the test passes it vacuously). DV-3 operator-authorised regression obligations: a confirmed failing test becomes a protected obligation only on operator authorisation; the original failure record is kept when later minimised | authorised obligation replayed on the next candidate and a weakening edit recorded; unauthorised promotion cannot happen | M | P2 |\n| VC-3 | **Done 2026-11-18.** Lineage (parent attempt, transition hash of the two candidate identities: not a content diff), the authorisation gate for a named reference (`/requirements reference`) and the behavioural differential (`/requirements differential <R> --inputs <file>`) are built. The reference is an operator-declared command; the candidate is the objective's latest verifier command; each declared input (JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) goes to both on stdin through the sandbox with no network, 30 s per run, output capped at 1 MiB; outputs are normalised with `normalizeOutcomeText` and compared with the exit code. A timeout, error or over-cap run is inconclusive for that input, never agreement. The result is finite agreement on the tested inputs (`agreementStatement`), never equivalence; there is no model-facing path. DV-4 attempt lineage (parent attempt, diff hash) and behavioural differential against a declared reference only when an authorised requirement names one (the Anthropic 2026 trends report's flagship long run, Rakuten's seven-hour single run at 99.9% numerical accuracy against a reference method, succeeded because a reference existed: the reference is what lets a long run stay autonomous. Raising this to P1 if MQ-1 shows reference-checkable tasks are common is an owner decision, not made here) | baseline-defect correction not blocked when an authoritative requirement contradicts the baseline; finite-case agreement never reported as equivalence | M | P2 |\n| VC-4 | **Done 2026-11-17:** the only pending scenario, `cumulative-change-03`, is active (pending count 0); `compaction-03` became active with CX-5. Close the two pending CERI scenarios once VC-1 lands; assign an owner to every scenario in `semantic_integrity` | pending count 0 or each pending carries a unit id | S | P1 |\n| VC-5 | Re-check `verified` on resume (invalidate when the candidate identity differs) and persist the unbound-pass marker so a resumed task does not hit the \"no matching baseline\" refusal | resume test: edited workspace demotes `verified`; resume after an unbound pass completes as REGRESSION_FREE | S | P1 |\n| VC-6 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/tester.ts`, `cli/src/core/completion_oracle.ts`, `cli/src/git.ts`, `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts`; manual section \"Type-check evidence\"). Baseline run (tsc, go vet, pyright, mypy; cargo excluded as not cheap) so an untouched caller broken by the change blocks VERIFIED; shell-changed files counted via the workspace diff; Go and Python (when configured); missing tool is UNAVAILABLE, never a pass; NEXUS_TYPECHECK=off unchanged. Limits: one check per project (first found: tsc, cargo, go, then python), no baseline after a resume, a baseline needs the verifier baseline to have run. Type-check gaps: baseline run to catch callers broken in untouched files, count shell-changed files as modified (workspace diff, not edit tools), Go (`go vet`) and Python (pyright/mypy when configured) | a change that breaks an untouched caller blocks VERIFIED; opt-out stays | M | P2 |\n| VC-7 | Convergence on the `run_tests` path and for operator-configured verifiers | a `run_tests`-only loop stops with the history attached | S | P2 **Done 2026-11-18** (`dispatch_tool_call.ts` judges `convergenceSignal` after a recorded verifier attempt; `cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`, the main case was red before the fix). An operator-configured verifier is covered because it is recorded through the same path. |\n| VC-8 | Per-role tool allowlists beyond reviewer and researcher (old backlog REQ:259, accepted 2026-11-15): allowlists for implementer and tester, consistent with SI-2's tester scoping (`roleToolAllowList`, `cli/src/agent.ts:5490`) | each role's disallowed tool is refused with a reason; existing flows unchanged | S | P2 (v1.2.2) |\n| VC-9 | **Partly done 2026-11-18** (audit: `docs/v1.2.1/VC-9-needs-input-audit.md`; glue: `cli/src/core/needs_input.ts`, `--answer`). Done: tool approval (write, exec, egress), verification-file edit, verifier and type-check approval end BLOCKED / NEEDS_INPUT with a question and resume with `--answer`. **Not done** (design decisions, see the audit): a requirement marked blocked (changes the REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE contract; free-text answer), an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar-only; resolve-after-the-fact), a subagent's question. Headless needs-input audit (Anthropic 2026 trends report, trend 4: agents that learn when to ask for help): list every path where a headless run needs an operator decision (steering that negates a requirement, a requirement marked blocked, a denied approval, a verification-file edit) and give each the same resumable outcome, a stable `needs_input` reason carrying the question and resumable with the answer; build only the missing glue | each path ends BLOCKED with a machine-readable question and resumes with an answer; no path guesses | S | P2 |\n| VC-10 | Steering semantic judge (optional; evidence in `docs/research/steering-judgment/`): a judge provider interface (none by default, the configured gateway, or an explicit opt-in third party) asked, for each live requirement, whether the steering message withdraws, defers or changes it; conflict (the existing operator decision) if the regex hits or `withdraws` >= 0.7 whatever `defers` says, advisory note between 0.3 and 0.7, `defers` and `changes` informational only; an unavailable judge records `semanticJudge: unavailable` and keeps the regex decision; the steer stays held from the model until the operator decides and no text goes to a third party unless opted in | **gated**: build only after a larger evaluation (at least 100 judgments from real steering messages, with the owner's consent, labelled blind, on a fresh held-out split) shows union recall of at least 0.8 with at most 5% false positives on the non-withdrawals, and after the synchronous path can hold a steer while a judge answers (the pilot gateway judge took a median of about 17 s per message) | M | P2 (v1.2.2, gated) |\n\n### CS: containment and security\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CS-1 | Containment by default: use the existing bubblewrap path by default when available (fall back to review mode with a visible notice when not); keep `--sandbox none` | decision D1; default flips only with an opt-out and a release-notes line; doctor reports the effective containment | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17 (D1: default stays opt-in):** doctor reports the effective containment and how to enable it (`cli/src/doctor.ts`). |\n| CS-2 | Hard stop for shell writes to verification files: after a `run_command`, compare the verification-file set against the pre-command snapshot and refuse completion (or revert, per decision D2) when it changed | a `sed -i Makefile` run ends BLOCKED with the diff; ordinary commands unaffected | M | P1 |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20014-b98e-7f50-9b44-1c38f2a1201a","create_time":1795297706.7996387}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":15897,"sampled_through_sequence":15894,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
