{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T21:49:02.546Z","ordinal":35656,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20014-b98e-7f50-9b44-1c38f2a1201a","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-eb1da542-1480-49ea-9732-3db18e3a9724","process_id":"29917","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git grep -n 'WP-P10-02\\|PA-1\\|PA-2\\|PA-3' origin/main -- docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md docs/v1.3.0/PA4* docs/v1.3.0 docs/evidence/v1.3.0 2>/dev/null | head -180; git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/v1.3.0 | rg 'PA[123]|P10|evidence|BASELINE' | head -100; find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 1 -mindepth 1 -type d -printf '%f\\n' | rg 'WP_P10|PA1|PA2|PA3|SUITE$|GATEWAY$'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git grep -n 'WP-P10-02\\|PA-1\\|PA-2\\|PA-3' origin/main -- docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md docs/v1.3.0/PA4* docs/v1.3.0 docs/evidence/v1.3.0 2>/dev/null | head -180; git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main docs/v1.3.0 | rg 'PA[123]|P10|evidence|BASELINE' | head -100; find .git/nexus-claims -maxdepth 1 -mindepth 1 -type d -printf '%f\\n' | rg 'WP_P10|PA1|PA2|PA3|SUITE$|GATEWAY$'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"origin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:1:# PA-1 explicit execution resource admission\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:3:Status: **partial implementation verified on the PA-1 resource-admission slice**. CPU/memory admission and compatibility behavior are implemented; worktree owner receipts and broader host-resource governance remain open.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:33:On branch `v1/pa1-resource-admission`, 44 focused tests passed across operation concurrency, operation schema, isolated concurrency, and batch spec validation; 28 operation release/review regression tests also passed. `npm run build` passed in `cli/`. The tests cover separate CPU and memory budget exhaustion, provider slot limits, malformed/missing reservation rejection, migration of old records, legacy serial behavior, and prior operation/batch regression cases. The release acceptance tests use an ephemeral local mock provider; no live inference endpoint was used. These results qualify this slice only, not the full PA-1 roadmap item.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:19:- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:20:- Operation records are versioned and reject records from a newer schema ([operation_schema.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts)). PA-1 resource reservations use schema v4; old records remain readable and schedule serially when their prior parallel declarations lack reservations.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:26:- `/batch` uses operation specifications and the existing `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`; it defaults to sequential execution. Parallel admission requires isolated worktrees and, as of PA-1 resource-admission work, explicit per-operation CPU/memory reservations. This adds no second scheduler or persisted workflow store.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:28:- Regression tests cover quoting/Unicode, workspace and symlink boundaries, sequential execution, durable state, partial aggregation, CPU/memory admission and legacy serial fallback. The PA-1 slice passed 44 focused operation/batch tests, 28 operation release/review tests, and the CLI TypeScript build in its isolated worktree. These are targeted results; they do not replace the prescribed full-suite qualification. No provider code changed.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:21:- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:94:| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:95:| PA-2 | Verified merge arbitration: when parallel units integrate, apply each diff, run verification on the merged tree, reject on failure. No peer read does more than isolate; this is where Nexus can differ | two units whose changes conflict semantically (each passes alone) fail verification on the merged tree and neither is integrated silently | M | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:96:| PA-3 | Goal semantics: a ledger entry active from an earlier process resumes **paused** (opencode `goal.ts` boot id), stall counting by failure fingerprint and not turn count, completion decided only by a verifier receipt, an optional token budget rolling up from sub-operations (reference: Codex goal budget); `/team` leaves experimental only after PA-1 and PA-2 | stale goal resumes paused with a visible reason; a model \"I am done\" does not complete a goal | M | P2 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:97:| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:102:Phase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:103:Phase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:104:Phase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:105:PA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md\nPA1_RESOURCE_BUDGETS_20261121\nGATEWAY\nWP_P10_02_DURABLE_BUDGET.released-20261119-rd7\nFILE_docs_v1.3.0_PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":32535}},"started_at_ms":1795297742545,"completed_at_ms":1795297742546}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T21:49:02.698Z","ordinal":35657,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a20015-8f6a-75e1-be72-85fa1737858f","call_id":"call_c90f3dde9b7f4fa08c29b20b87cead58","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"origin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:1:# PA-1 explicit execution resource admission\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:3:Status: **partial implementation verified on the PA-1 resource-admission slice**. CPU/memory admission and compatibility behavior are implemented; worktree owner receipts and broader host-resource governance remain open.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md:33:On branch `v1/pa1-resource-admission`, 44 focused tests passed across operation concurrency, operation schema, isolated concurrency, and batch spec validation; 28 operation release/review regression tests also passed. `npm run build` passed in `cli/`. The tests cover separate CPU and memory budget exhaustion, provider slot limits, malformed/missing reservation rejection, migration of old records, legacy serial behavior, and prior operation/batch regression cases. The release acceptance tests use an ephemeral local mock provider; no live inference endpoint was used. These results qualify this slice only, not the full PA-1 roadmap item.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:19:- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:20:- Operation records are versioned and reject records from a newer schema ([operation_schema.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts)). PA-1 resource reservations use schema v4; old records remain readable and schedule serially when their prior parallel declarations lack reservations.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:26:- `/batch` uses operation specifications and the existing `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`; it defaults to sequential execution. Parallel admission requires isolated worktrees and, as of PA-1 resource-admission work, explicit per-operation CPU/memory reservations. This adds no second scheduler or persisted workflow store.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:28:- Regression tests cover quoting/Unicode, workspace and symlink boundaries, sequential execution, durable state, partial aggregation, CPU/memory admission and legacy serial fallback. The PA-1 slice passed 44 focused operation/batch tests, 28 operation release/review tests, and the CLI TypeScript build in its isolated worktree. These are targeted results; they do not replace the prescribed full-suite qualification. No provider code changed.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:21:- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:94:| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:95:| PA-2 | Verified merge arbitration: when parallel units integrate, apply each diff, run verification on the merged tree, reject on failure. No peer read does more than isolate; this is where Nexus can differ | two units whose changes conflict semantically (each passes alone) fail verification on the merged tree and neither is integrated silently | M | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:96:| PA-3 | Goal semantics: a ledger entry active from an earlier process resumes **paused** (opencode `goal.ts` boot id), stall counting by failure fingerprint and not turn count, completion decided only by a verifier receipt, an optional token budget rolling up from sub-operations (reference: Codex goal budget); `/team` leaves experimental only after PA-1 and PA-2 | stale goal resumes paused with a visible reason; a model \"I am done\" does not complete a goal | M | P2 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:97:| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:102:Phase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:103:Phase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:104:Phase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:105:PA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\ndocs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md\nPA1_RESOURCE_BUDGETS_20261121\nGATEWAY\nWP_P10_02_DURABLE_BUDGET.released-20261119-rd7\nFILE_docs_v1.3.0_PA1_RESOURCE_ADMISSION.md\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20014-b98e-7f50-9b44-1c38f2a1201a","create_time":1795297742.698007}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":15912,"sampled_through_sequence":15909,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
