{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:44.229Z","ordinal":36569,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","status":"completed","id":"msg_0393e1fe95dcae93016b021c831fd087d28d4102cc6cdc8cc7","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I’m resuming the full v1.3.0 implementation goal. First I’m checking the merged CI result, the current roadmap and claims, and the active worktree state so the next implementation slice follows the repository’s dependency order.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","create_time":1795300483.1261902,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":16343,"sampled_through_sequence":16341,"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_0393e1fe95dcae93016b021c831fd087d28d4102cc6cdc8cc7","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a2003f-6485-7962-8ce0-f2e5212559d9","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":1361}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:48.724Z","ordinal":36570,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0393e1fe95dcae93016b021c84346087d280d0a713cbb0fd36","status":"completed","call_id":"call_73827278c8ab4d758fd670db8d5217e0","name":"exec","input":"const results = await Promise.allSettled([\n  tools.get_goal({}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"git status --short --branch && git worktree list --porcelain && git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main && gh run view 44956866625 --repo mikeholownych/nexus-code --json status,conclusion,jobs --jq '{status, conclusion, jobs: [.jobs[] | {name, status, conclusion, running: [.steps[] | select(.status == \\\"in_progress\\\") | .name]}]}'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":1500}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":5000})\n]);\nfor (const r of results) text(JSON.stringify(r));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","create_time":1795300484.2063768}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":16344,"sampled_through_sequence":16341,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:48.765Z","ordinal":36571,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","session_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","root_turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","response_id":"resp_0393e1fe95dcae93016b021c7f00c087d2855425e2bfd4101d","usage":{"input_tokens":66687,"cached_input_tokens":65024,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":503,"reasoning_output_tokens":188,"total_tokens":67190},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":66687,"cached_input_tokens":65024,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":503,"reasoning_output_tokens":188,"total_tokens":67190},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":779751565,"cached_input_tokens":772802432,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1140910,"reasoning_output_tokens":519732,"total_tokens":780892475}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:48.906Z","ordinal":36572,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5c9682ec-4e55-4dd2-b6cb-e0119f0a0b6f","process_id":"90204","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md","name":"PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md","name":"PA4_BASELINE.md","path":"docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md","name":"CONCURRENCY.md","path":"docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Nexus Code v1.3 plan: the platform track\n\nStatus: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\n\n## 1. Purpose and theme\n\nFrontier clients are platforms: a CLI plus a desktop app, IDE integration, background and scheduled agents, an SDK or managed API, installable plugin bundles, and enterprise policy (web sources of 2026-11-15, mostly third-party; see [`docs/v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](../v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md) §2). Nexus is a single-binary Linux CLI and TUI. It cannot match breadth or first-party model fit, and a worse copy of three funded platforms is not a goal.\n\nThe v1.3 theme: **let other programs drive Nexus and let teams govern it, without weakening the gate.** Every new surface (an IDE over ACP, a scheduler, a hook, a plugin, an SDK caller, a managed policy) goes through the same approval and verification gates as the terminal does. The differentiator to build on is the one nobody else ships by default: a completion you can audit (independent verification, receipts, evidence). Two consequences shape the plan:\n\n1. **Nexus as the verification layer for other harnesses.** `nexus verify` and an MCP/ACP `verify` tool let a Codex, Claude Code or Aider session ask Nexus to independently check its work. This costs little (the machinery exists) and is positioned where the platforms are weakest.\n2. **Governance as a product.** Managed policy with fixed precedence, a hash-chained and optionally signed audit export, and hooks that can only tighten.\n\nNon-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\n\n## 2. Evidence and its limits\n\n- **Nexus today** (read-only inventory, 2026-11-15; \"present\" means present in source or docs, nothing was run): sidecar/TUI protocol is NDJSON over the sidecar's stdio with `PROTOCOL_VERSION = 1`, an event `v`, a one-way `hello`, typed unions in `cli/src/protocol.ts:53-204` hand-mirrored in `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/protocol.rs`, **no schema file, no socket**; `nexus run --json` returns `TaskExecutionResult` (`cli/src/core/app.ts:44`) with no schema or version, exit codes defined only in code (0 verified, 2 completed-unverified, 130 interrupted, 1 otherwise); `ApplicationCore` is an accidental re-export (`cli/src/index.ts:1240`) and `cli/package.json` has `main` and `bin` but no `exports` or `types`; operations are durable and resumable (per-operation lock, 12 h wait survives restart, `maxConcurrentUnits` capped at 64 and clamped to 1 without `git.isolate`) but **foreground-only**, with no scheduler, cron, trigger or daemon in the code; plugins are plain JS modules with no manifest, checksum or install command, workspace plugins load after `nexus trust` with full process authority, hooks cover pre/post tool only; policy is the workspace-local `.nexus-security.json`, **fails open to defaults on a parse error**, with no system or admin layer; session export is `nexus-session-export/2` reachable only through `/export`; the flight log is append-only by convention (no hash chain found) and the CERI evidence store hashes files but has no signature or verify command; local OTLP-style traces are written on every session with no opt-in gate and no network exporter found; a goal (objective ledger) persists across sessions via `--resume`, but nothing re-launches a task on its own.\n- **Peers, read from source** (shallow clones of 2026-11-13/14; Codex at commit 336b19a; untrusted code, nothing run): ACP servers in goose (`crates/goose/src/acp`), Cline (`apps/cli/src/acp/acpAgent.ts`), Gemini CLI (`packages/cli/src/acp`) and opencode (`packages/cli/src/acp/agent.ts`), the Codex `app-server` (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, websocket or UDS; schema generated from Rust types and checked in); schedulers in goose (`scheduler/full.rs`), Cline (`*.cron.md` and event specs) and opencode (a `ScheduleTable` with `in`/`at`/`every`/`cron` and an optional deterministic wake `check`); hooks in Gemini CLI (`docs/hooks/reference.md`), goose (`hooks/mod.rs`), Cline and Codex; extension manifests in Gemini CLI (`gemini-extension.json`), goose (Open Plugins and Gemini formats) and opencode; policy tiers in Gemini CLI (`core/src/policy/config.ts:67-71`), opencode (`managed-policy.ts`) and Codex (`requirements.toml`); goal state in opencode (`schema/src/goal.ts`) and Codex (`thread/goal/*`).\n- **XERJ reference queries** (2026-11-15, local node, read-only; the `opencode` index is flagged \"not verified complete\", so a miss there is not proof of absence): `xerj code opencode` for ACP, scheduling and plugin manifests; `xerj search --prefix goose` for ACP, scheduler and extension manifests; `xerj search --prefix xc-opencode` for managed policy. **Took:** opencode's wire-level ACP fixture pattern (`packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) for protocol conformance tests; opencode's schedule model (`schema/src/schedule.ts`, `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`: `next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome`, deterministic wake check with hard limits) and its documented GitHub scheduled-workflow example; goose's cron-on-a-recipe scheduler shape (`scheduler/full.rs`, `commands/schedule.rs`); goose's checked-in ACP schema and method metadata (`acp-meta.json`); goose's plugin formats (`plugins/formats/gemini.rs`, `open_plugins.rs`); opencode's managed policy as config statements with an action, a resource and an effect (`config/plugin/policy.ts`). **Rejected:** opencode's in-process Effect plugin hooks (a JS-in-process model is what Nexus is trying to move away from), goose's per-run `Auto` mode for scheduled runs (unattended runs must never get allow-always), and any code copied verbatim: reference code is read-only inspiration.\n- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\n- **Not covered:** Claude Code and agy were not read (web sources only); parts of Cline's daemon, Codex's hook handler types and plugin manifest, and goose's managed policy were sampled, not read in full; peer behaviour under load was not measured.\n\n## 3. Principles\n\n1. **Protocol first.** Nothing else in this plan is safe to build until the client protocol is published, schema'd, versioned and conformance-tested.\n2. **One gate.** A new surface can request an approval but can never answer one on behalf of Nexus's own gates (SI-2, CS-* rules, verification). A client that answers \"allow\" to a denied action must have no effect; this is tested.\n3. **Tighten only.** Hooks and managed policy may deny, require a human, or rewrite arguments toward safer values. They never grant.\n4. **Unattended means stricter.** Scheduled and detached runs never get allow-always, run in a fresh worktree, and record that they were unattended in every receipt.\n5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\n6. **Schemas come from types.** Generate JSON Schema from the TypeScript types and check it in with a parity test, so the Rust mirror cannot drift (reference: Codex `app-server-protocol` schema export, goose `acp-schema.json`).\n7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\n8. **Experimental is a flag.** New methods sit behind `experimental` at `initialize` (reference: Codex `experimentalApi`) until a conformance suite has run against two real clients.\n\n## 4. Workstreams and units\n\nSize: S = one PR; M = two to four PRs; L = a program. P0 = prerequisite, P1 = core, P2 = stretch.\n\n### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\n| PF-2 | `nexus run --json` result schema with a `schema_version`, documented exit codes in the manual, `--input-json` or stdin for the task | schema validated in tests; a consumer test parses the output | S | P0 |\n| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\n| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\n\n### AC: ACP and IDE access\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AC-1 | ACP adapter over the sidecar protocol (stdio), hand-rolled JSON-RPC with no dependency: `initialize` with a capability list, `session/new`, `session/load`, `session/prompt`, `session/cancel`; streaming `tool_call` and `tool_call_update` with diff content; a `verification` update carrying receipt ids and verifier results; client-bound `session/request_permission` with allow-once and reject only (no \"allow always\" at first); one extension, `_nexus/receipt/get`. Reference: goose, Cline, opencode, Gemini CLI ACP servers and the Codex app-server (all four ACP servers use the same three-option permission request; opencode attaches the diff to the request so the user decides with the change in view). The host side exists too: OpenHands' Agent Canvas drives Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode over ACP (docs, 2026-11-15), so an ACP server is what makes Nexus drivable by such hosts | a scripted ACP client completes a task; an `allow` reply to an action the gates deny has no effect (gate test G-3); the receipt is retrievable | L | P1 |\n| AC-2 | ACP conformance: fixture suite plus runs against two real ACP clients (decision D14), recorded as fixtures; no live model | both clients complete the basic flow; fixtures committed | M | P1 |\n\nThis supersedes v1.2.1 unit RD-5 (ACP server mode), which moves here.\n\n### VR: Nexus as the verification layer\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| VR-1 | `nexus verify` (no agent run): given a worktree or a base and head, run the discovered or configured verifier, the type-check evidence (CG-4), the verification-file guard and the independent reviewer, and print a verdict VERIFIED / REGRESSION_FREE / UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED with the receipt; exit code per the documented contract; usable as a CI gate (pairs with the v1.2.1 GitHub Action, RD-4) | a known-bad diff (weakened test) is not VERIFIED; the same diff produced by another harness gives the same verdict | M | P1 |\n| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\n\n### BG: background and scheduled runs\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BG-1 | Detached runner: a user-level job runner that owns durable operations so a run survives its CLI process (decision D15: a documented `systemd --user` unit versus a self-daemonizing mode); foreground mode unchanged | kill the CLI, the run continues and resumes cleanly; the SI-7 kill test passes against the runner | L | P1 |\n| BG-2 | Schedules: a spec (trigger `cron`, `every` or `once`; objective ledger id; `max_rounds`; timeout; policy `read-only` or `worktree-write`; optional deterministic wake `check` command with hard limits, reference opencode `schedule/check.ts:23-39`: 30 s, 20 tool calls, 32 KB, cannot create schedules or worktrees) stored beside operations (`next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome` as in opencode `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`); `nexus schedule add\\|list\\|rm\\|run-now`; each run in a fresh worktree; unattended flag in every receipt; no allow-always; delivery is a receipt plus a pointer file | a scheduled run on a seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED and says so; an unattended run asking for approval is denied, not blocked forever; missed runs are reported, not silently skipped | L | P1 |\n| BG-3 | Event triggers (file change, webhook) | deferred: needs a trust and abuse model first | M | P2 (v1.4) |\n\n### GV: governance\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GV-1 | Managed policy with fixed precedence **system > user > project**: `/etc/nexus/policy.toml` (path per decision D16), `~/.config/nexus/policy.toml`, then the existing `.nexus-security.json`; restrict-only semantics (deny, require a human, disable auto/yolo, allowlist MCP servers and plugins, set verification-path modes); the effective tier is printed beside every decision; a parse error in the system or user tier **fails closed**; a project policy file is hashed and a changed one asks again (reference: Gemini CLI tiers, MATCH/MISMATCH/NEW integrity check and system settings paths; opencode managed policy; Codex `requirements.toml`) | a project policy cannot loosen a system policy; a corrupted system policy refuses to start with a clear message instead of failing open; precedence table covered by tests | M | P1 |\n| GV-2 | Audit export: `nexus audit export <task\\|session>` writes a bundle (session export, receipts, evidence, verification attempts, flight log excerpt) with a manifest of SHA-256 hashes and a hash chain, optionally signed with an operator-provided ed25519 key (`node:crypto`, decision D17); `nexus audit verify` | a bundle with one altered byte fails verification and names the file; an unsigned bundle verifies by hash only and says so | M | P1 |\n| GV-3 | Hash-chain the flight recorder and the verification-attempt log (today append-only by convention only) and verify the chain on `nexus flights replay` | truncating or editing a record is detected | S | P1 |\n| GV-4 | Telemetry transparency (this also settles v1.2.1 unit PC-5): document what the local traces contain, add a setting to disable them and a retention limit, state plainly that nothing leaves the host (no network exporter found) | doc plus a test that no network call is made | S | P1 |\n| GV-5 | Admin controls from the system policy: a strict mode that disables auto and full-access, an MCP and plugin allowlist | strict mode refuses `--yes` and `--mode auto` with the policy source named | S | P1 |\n\n### HK: hooks and plugins\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HK-1 | Declarative command hooks: events `pre-tool`, `post-tool`, `pre-verify`, `post-verify`, `permission-request`, `session-start`, `session-end`, `stop`, `pre-compact`; JSON on stdin (`session_id`, `cwd`, `hook_event_name`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`) and on stdout (`decision`, `reason`, `hookSpecificOutput`), exit 2 blocks with stderr as the reason, a timeout, a scrubbed environment (reuse `buildMcpEnvironment`), results written to the receipt. A hook may only deny or tighten arguments, never grant (Gemini CLI also ignores `allow` from lower tiers). Project-defined hooks need a trust decision keyed by a hash of the command (reference: Gemini CLI `trusted_hooks.json`, goose `hooks/mod.rs`, Codex hook trust). Handler types: command only (no peer shipped http, prompt or subagent handlers in what was read) | a hook that returns `allow` for a denied action has no effect; a changed project hook asks again; the contract is documented so existing hook scripts of that shape transfer | M | P1 |\n| HK-2 | Plugin manifest `nexus-plugin.json` (name, version, declared contributions: hooks, skills, verifiers, MCP servers; required permissions); install from a local path or a git URL **pinned to a commit hash**; capabilities shown at install, resolved commit recorded in an install receipt; `nexus plugins install\\|list\\|remove\\|verify`; no marketplace and no auto-update (reference: Gemini CLI `gemini-extension.json`, goose `.goose-plugin-install.json`). Skills load on a declared trigger and are not always-on (references: OpenHands trigger-based skills, the on-demand-loading guidance of `agent-rules-books`). Also fix the documented example in `docs/SKILLS_AND_PLUGINS.md` that does not match the code (`onTurnStart` positional arguments versus a context object) and stop swallowing plugin load errors silently | installing a plugin whose declared permissions exceed the system policy is refused; `verify` detects a modified install; a skill whose trigger did not fire adds nothing to the request (request-shape snapshot) | M | P1 |\n| HK-3 | Per-plugin trust instead of per-directory, and run workspace-defined plugin hooks as subprocesses rather than in-process (a trusted workspace today runs every `.js` file in it with full process authority) | decision D18; migration note for existing plugins | M | P2 |\n\n### PA: parallel agents and goals\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\n| PA-2 | Verified merge arbitration: when parallel units integrate, apply each diff, run verification on the merged tree, reject on failure. No peer read does more than isolate; this is where Nexus can differ | two units whose changes conflict semantically (each passes alone) fail verification on the merged tree and neither is integrated silently | M | P1 |\n| PA-3 | Goal semantics: a ledger entry active from an earlier process resumes **paused** (opencode `goal.ts` boot id), stall counting by failure fingerprint and not turn count, completion decided only by a verifier receipt, an optional token budget rolling up from sub-operations (reference: Codex goal budget); `/team` leaves experimental only after PA-1 and PA-2 | stale goal resumes paused with a visible reason; a model \"I am done\" does not complete a goal | M | P2 |\n| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\n\n## 5. Sequencing\n\nPhase A (foundation, parallelisable): PF-1, PF-2, GV-4, VR-1, GV-3. These unblock the rest and cost little.\nPhase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2.\nPhase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5.\nPhase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\nPA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\n\nDependencies on earlier plans: v1.2.1 RD-3 (`stream-json` output) feeds PF-2; RD-4 (GitHub Action) pairs with VR-1; CS-9 (per-command approval rules, v1.2.2) and GV-1 must agree on rule semantics (allow < prompt < forbidden, deciding rule recorded); MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split) makes AC-1 and BG-1 much easier and should land first if possible; PR-* (provider registry) is independent; MQ-* (measurement) should publish at least one result before this release claims anything.\n\n## 6. Release gates\n\n| Gate | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| G-1 | `make test` green at the exact head; required CI green; main CI green on the merge commit |\n| G-2 | Protocol conformance: schema parity test and the wire-fixture suite green; AC-1 passes against two real clients or the release says \"experimental\" |\n| G-3 | **Gate-bypass suite:** a hostile client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy file cannot approve a denied action, raise a verdict, widen a policy tier, or remove a verification-file protection |\n| G-4 | Unattended runs: tests show no allow-always, a fresh worktree, the unattended flag in receipts, and a kill-and-resume pass |\n| G-5 | Audit round trip: export, verify, tamper one byte, verify fails and names the file |\n| G-6 | Docs and examples for every new surface (`docs/PROTOCOL.md`, hooks, plugins, policy, schedules, audit) with runnable examples in CI |\n| G-7 | Notes keep the framing: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" unless an external result (E2 per the v1.2.1 plan §7) exists |\n\n## 7. Decisions needed\n\n| Id | Decision | Default if unanswered |\n|---|---|---|\n| D13 | Publish an npm package or SDK (PF-4)? Package name and licence; external publishing is yours to authorise | no publish; the entry point is documented but not published |\n| D14 | Which two ACP clients to test against (AC-2) | the two most widely used open-source ones that run on Linux without an account |\n| D15 | Detached runner model: a documented `systemd --user` unit, or a self-daemonizing mode (BG-1) | the `systemd --user` unit only |\n| D16 | System policy location and whether the system and user tiers fail closed on a parse error (GV-1) | `/etc/nexus/policy.toml`; fail closed |\n| D17 | Audit signing: where the operator's ed25519 key lives and whether signing is required or optional (GV-2) | optional; key path supplied by the operator, never stored by Nexus |\n| D18 | Whether in-process workspace JS plugins stay supported after HK-1/HK-2 (HK-3) | keep, but move to per-plugin trust with a deprecation note |\n| D19 | Unattended-run defaults (BG-2): `read-only` only, or `worktree-write` allowed | `read-only` by default; `worktree-write` per schedule with an explicit flag |\n| D20 | Position Nexus as a verifier for other harnesses (VR-2)? | yes |\n| D21 | Event triggers and remote (SSH) execution: v1.4 or never | v1.4 candidate, no work in v1.3 |\n\n## 8. Risks\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| A new socket or listener widens the attack surface | unix socket mode 0600 by default; token and origin check for any HTTP listener; G-3 |\n| A client answers approvals in a way that bypasses a gate | the gate decides first; the client only supplies a human's yes or no; G-3 tests it |\n| Fail-closed policy locks an operator out | a clear message naming the file and tier, and a documented recovery path; tested |\n| Unattended runs burn the shared gateway or contend with live measurements | schedules use the `GATEWAY` claim protocol; read-only default; run budget in every spec |\n| Plugin supply chain | commit-pinned installs, capabilities shown, no auto-update, install receipt, `verify` |\n| Protocol churn after publication | `experimental` flag, version range in `hello`, schema diff in CI |\n| Scope: this track is as large as v1.2.0 plus v1.2.1 | the sequencing above; Phase D rolls without ceremony; a unit is never half-merged |\n| An unreviewed peer assumption | sources and limits are listed in section 2; each unit's PR repeats the reference coding record (XERJ queries, what was taken or rejected) |\n# PA-4 baseline: delegation and workflow execution\n\n**Snapshot:** Nexus `0151bef3a8a95e3cd3df2d632439b30811e6415c` (`origin/main`), inspected 2026-11-21. This is a source and test inventory; source presence and test-file names are not runtime verification, and the existing tests listed here were not run for this baseline.\n\n| Area | Status | Evidence and observed boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interactive turns and inference | Implemented | `AutonomousAgent.runTurn` delegates to `runTurnImpl` ([agent.ts:4186](../../cli/src/agent.ts#L4186), [run_turn.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/run_turn.ts#L26)); provider recovery and context admission are in the turn path. `TurnResult` distinguishes interrupted, blocked and failed executions and requires evidence for verified outcomes ([turn.ts:17](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L17), [turn.ts:423](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L423)). |\n| Transient delegation | Partially implemented; qualification pending | `runSubagent` remains the in-turn child path ([agent.ts](../../cli/src/agent.ts)); `spawn_subagent` now selects a predefined profile, narrows tools at schema and dispatcher boundaries, clears inherited full access, and emits a typed receipt ([spine_handlers.ts](../../cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts), [dispatch_tool_call.ts](../../cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts)). Prompt-only calls default to read-only `semantic_explorer`; writable profiles require an established worktree. Depth and per-parent child-attempt limits are enforced. Process-wide resource budgets, full task/session/workspace grant derivation, deterministic security tests, and independent evidence review remain outstanding; see [transient authority milestone](PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md).\n| Durable work units and scheduling | Implemented | `newOperation` constructs operation state ([operation.ts:502](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L502)) and the graph rejects dependency cycles ([operation.ts:582](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L582)); `ConvergenceScheduler` selects transitions ([scheduler.ts:53](../../cli/src/core/scheduler.ts#L53)); `driveOperation` and `OperationStore` persist and resume work ([operation_runner.ts:717](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts#L717), [operation_runner_persistence.ts:28](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner_persistence.ts#L28)). Existing tests cover schema, restart, concurrency and isolated integration.\n| Roles and independent review | Implemented, incomplete | Specialist profiles define permitted tools, read-only declarations, inputs, ceilings and output contracts ([specialist_profiles.ts:22](../../cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts#L22)); operation roles include team and specialist profiles ([roles.ts:101](../../cli/src/core/roles.ts#L101)). Independent-review formatting and verdict validation exist ([independent_review.ts:131](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L131), [independent_review.ts:204](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L204)); source presence does not establish all requested independence dimensions or reviewer behavior under truncated evidence.\n| Resource and provider handling | Implemented, incomplete | `ResourceRegistry` handles eligibility and provider conditions ([resources.ts:194](../../cli/src/core/resources.ts#L194)); provider capability states include `unknown` and `unavailable` ([provider_capabilities.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/provider_capabilities.ts#L26)); recovery classification/controller exists ([provider_recovery.ts:96](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L96), [provider_recovery.ts:367](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L367)). Provider test sources include `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts`, `provider_capabilities.test.ts`, `provider_robustness.test.ts`, `cloud_providers.test.ts` and `provider_recovery.test.ts`; this assignment's complete workflow has not been run against the requested minimal contract fixture.\n| `/team` and operation CLI | Implemented, incomplete | `TeamOrchestrator` maps roles to durable work units ([team.ts:43](../../cli/src/team.ts#L43), [team.ts:126](../../cli/src/team.ts#L126)); the operation CLI accepts validated JSON work-unit specs ([operation_cli.ts:106](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts#L106)). The REPL has a dedicated `/team` handler ([repl.ts:1702](../../cli/src/repl.ts#L1702)); the sidecar routes commands through a separate slash router ([router.ts:14](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts#L14)). TUI completion/help comes from the slash catalogue ([catalog.ts:138](../../cli/src/slash/catalog.ts#L138)), with remote invocation parsing in Rust ([slash.rs:25](../../core/src/bin/nexus_tui/slash.rs#L25)).\n| `/loop`, `/goal`, `/batch` | `/batch` implemented, `/loop` and `/goal` unimplemented | `/batch` is present in the command catalogue and routes to `BatchOrchestrator`; it validates existing operation JSON specs and persists through `OperationStore` and `OperationRunner` ([batch.ts](../../cli/src/batch.ts), [batch_spec.ts](../../cli/src/batch_spec.ts), [router.ts](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts), [repl.ts](../../cli/src/repl.ts)). `batch.test.ts` and `batch_spec.test.ts` cover command behavior. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycle controllers remain absent; loop detector tests do not implement durable loop state, and the objective ledger has no `/goal` lifecycle.\n| Pause, cancellation and restart | Implemented, incomplete | Durable operations include distinct `PAUSED`, cancellation and bounded restart reconciliation ([operation.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts), [operation_runner.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts), [operation_cli.ts](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts)). `/batch` pause is honored at a safe boundary. Unknown effects still need stronger reconciliation semantics for the broader delegation assignment; `/loop` lifecycle recovery is absent.\n| Skills and authority | Implemented, incomplete | Skill activation has constraints and content digests ([skill_activation.ts:67](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L67), [skill_activation.ts:104](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L104)). Capability registration exists ([capabilities.ts:120](../../cli/src/core/capabilities.ts#L120)) and the orchestration capability schema declares `spawn_subagent` ([orchestration.ts:38](../../cli/src/core/capability_definitions/orchestration.ts#L38)). Full effect-path mediation and path-scoped profile/skill eligibility still require adversarial tests from the assignment.\n\n## Roadmap and compatibility notes\n\n- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\n- Operation records are versioned and reject records from a newer schema ([operation_schema.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts)). PA-1 resource reservations use schema v4; old records remain readable and schedule serially when their prior parallel declarations lack reservations.\n- XERJ definition-first lookup identified `TeamOrchestrator`, `WorkUnitSpec`, `newOperation` and the existing dependency-cycle check. The first batch-spec package reuses the operation parser and constructor; no orchestration code was copied.\n- No provider, gateway, or homelab-ai change is part of the workstream.\n\n## Implementation progress (2026-11-21)\n\n- `/batch` uses operation specifications and the existing `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`; it defaults to sequential execution. Parallel admission requires isolated worktrees and, as of PA-1 resource-admission work, explicit per-operation CPU/memory reservations. This adds no second scheduler or persisted workflow store.\n- `/batch` is wired into the sidecar slash router and REPL, so the TUI obtains its completion/help entry from the same slash catalogue. The implemented actions are `run`, `validate`, `status`, `list`, `pause`, `resume`, `cancel`, and `results`. Pause writes the durable `PAUSED` status at a safe boundary. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycles remain unimplemented.\n- Regression tests cover quoting/Unicode, workspace and symlink boundaries, sequential execution, durable state, partial aggregation, CPU/memory admission and legacy serial fallback. The PA-1 slice passed 44 focused operation/batch tests, 28 operation release/review tests, and the CLI TypeScript build in its isolated worktree. These are targeted results; they do not replace the prescribed full-suite qualification. No provider code changed.\n- A definition-first XERJ lookup in the `opencode` corpus found `packages/schema/src/goal.ts:11` (MIT) with bounded round count, stall count, and boot identity fields. This informed the roadmap comparison only; no peer code was copied. The corpus is flagged as incompletely indexed, and the listed `goose` corpus was unavailable.\n- This is an implementation checkpoint, not completion evidence for PA-4. Authority enforcement, prompt-only runtime compatibility tests, distinct pause semantics, `/loop`, `/goal`, full batch terminal-state persistence, adversarial coverage, interface-level behavior tests, minimal-provider workflow fixtures, independent review, and comparative evaluation remain outstanding.\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n|---|---|\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n\n## 8. Independence constraints\n\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":46808}},"started_at_ms":1795300488904,"completed_at_ms":1795300488905}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:50.194Z","ordinal":36573,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-f42c4e70-be01-4bc0-8777-4a6f0817e705","process_id":"15068","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git status --short --branch && git worktree list --porcelain && git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main && gh run view 44956866625 --repo mikeholownych/nexus-code --json status,conclusion,jobs --jq '{status, conclusion, jobs: [.jobs[] | {name, status, conclusion, running: [.steps[] | select(.status == \"in_progress\") | .name]}]}'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git status --short --branch && git worktree list --porcelain && git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main && gh run view 44956866625 --repo mikeholownych/nexus-code --json status,conclusion,jobs --jq '{status, conclusion, jobs: [.jobs[] | {name, status, conclusion, running: [.steps[] | select(.status == \"in_progress\") | .name]}]}'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"## main...origin/main [behind 12]\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-code\nHEAD 4f553c240d7941edeea43e65979dcf4dd50aba99\nbranch refs/heads/main\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/AUDIT_skills_architecture_2026-11-18\nHEAD 3c54e6014c2ea5281f642fdd53ac20605f2b24ae\nbranch refs/heads/audit/skills-architecture-2026-11-18\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/cs10b\nHEAD b010bad054b318b3283a7c0c1b96e0714c44c737\nbranch refs/heads/v1/cs10-docker-files-worker\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/g114-v122rc1\nHEAD 2d314098d8a5b6d9863d22f791007b27f65d2956\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/g114ev\nHEAD c9e6be0151d85f897d91a6f18193c62ad707f04c\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-rc1-g114-evidence\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/mq4-repeat\nHEAD b0d59908e75a51d57449e17bd348068713f2e567\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/mq4-second-run-evidence\nHEAD 9545f1bb5bef7af22852e549b7f1ee106c6ebe9a\nbranch refs/heads/codex/mq4-second-run-evidence\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-qualify-29182fee\nHEAD 29182fee80351aeb85d9e2de36a0870aa8218b77\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-qualify-8526b2a3\nHEAD 8526b2a3e36bbd5c56987405e16c810682f2ce31\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-resource-admission\nHEAD 29182fee80351aeb85d9e2de36a0870aa8218b77\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa1-resource-admission\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-orchestration\nHEAD f0b61e05637da9ba45a8715df6e6861b1d38c1b9\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa4-orchestration\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-transient-contract-tests\nHEAD 1efb86255b7c0cd685bec1f24b0eb8b72a32e94b\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa4-transient-contract-tests\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-validation\nHEAD a4249461206ba30c86dc53a0d0b43a383bede07f\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/rc1\nHEAD c1fcafa62e669f26c2a289b20e35cf984f53e20b\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-rc1\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v122-rc1-tag\nHEAD 2d314098d8a5b6d9863d22f791007b27f65d2956\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v122stable\nHEAD ce2c951e249d581e97dd19bcf1d771de62eb5157\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-stable\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-colorize-tui\nHEAD 073e6ea90b025acbd19f0c1b92ea1c9e93e6937e\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v130-colorize-tui\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-tui-audit-fixes\nHEAD 93ecfdd2129f753fc0a9a7c11013eaea5eaafa5f\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v130-tui-audit-fixes\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-tui-audit-suite\nHEAD ca300407effd7ece89ba60d86f57c058f109b5bc\ndetached\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc6-cargo\nHEAD 82938c303364edbbb41934bd73ab5a6655e82f2c\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v12x-vc6-cargo-baseline\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc9-investigation\nHEAD c674b17683881c7fcd1e553c44932396c0f62fb7\nbranch refs/heads/v1/vc9-persistence-identity\n\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm3-repeat\nHEAD b0d59908e75a51d57449e17bd348068713f2e567\ndetached\n\na5fa1dca54c176e50f16176fe82454a2dff360e6\trefs/heads/main\n{\"conclusion\":\"\",\"jobs\":[{\"conclusion\":\"success\",\"name\":\"cloudflare-experiment (24)\",\"running\":[],\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"conclusion\":\"\",\"name\":\"test-matrix (24)\",\"running\":[\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\"],\"status\":\"in_progress\"},{\"conclusion\":\"\",\"name\":\"test-matrix (22)\",\"running\":[\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\"],\"status\":\"in_progress\"},{\"conclusion\":\"success\",\"name\":\"cloudflare-experiment (22)\",\"running\":[],\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"conclusion\":\"\",\"name\":\"linux-x86_64\",\"running\":[\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\"],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":214347403}},"started_at_ms":1795300488979,"completed_at_ms":1795300490194}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:34:50.333Z","ordinal":36574,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a2003f-7c5d-7730-9bad-a3068d05b066","call_id":"call_73827278c8ab4d758fd670db8d5217e0","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"goal\":{\"threadId\":\"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e\",\"objective\":\"resume\",\"status\":\"active\",\"tokensUsed\":5008705,\"timeUsedSeconds\":72679,\"createdAt\":1795227799,\"updatedAt\":1795300478},\"remainingTokens\":null,\"completionBudgetReport\":null}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"9f5634\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.214941426,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":840,\"output\":\"## main...origin/main [behind 12]\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-code\\nHEAD 4f553c240d7941edeea43e65979dcf4dd50aba99\\nbranch refs/heads/main\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/AUDIT_skills_architecture_2026-11-18\\nHEAD 3c54e6014c2ea5281f642fdd53ac20605f2b24ae\\nbranch refs/heads/audit/skills-architecture-2026-11-18\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/cs10b\\nHEAD b010bad054b318b3283a7c0c1b96e0714c44c737\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/cs10-docker-files-worker\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/g114-v122rc1\\nHEAD 2d314098d8a5b6d9863d22f791007b27f65d2956\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/g114ev\\nHEAD c9e6be0151d85f897d91a6f18193c62ad707f04c\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-rc1-g114-evidence\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/mq4-repeat\\nHEAD b0d59908e75a51d57449e17bd348068713f2e567\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/mq4-second-run-evidence\\nHEAD 9545f1bb5bef7af22852e549b7f1ee106c6ebe9a\\nbranch refs/heads/codex/mq4-second-run-evidence\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-qualify-29182fee\\nHEAD 29182fee80351aeb85d9e2de36a0870aa8218b77\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-qualify-8526b2a3\\nHEAD 8526b2a3e36bbd5c56987405e16c810682f2ce31\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa1-resource-admission\\nHEAD 29182fee80351aeb85d9e2de36a0870aa8218b77\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa1-resource-admission\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-orchestration\\nHEAD f0b61e05637da9ba45a8715df6e6861b1d38c1b9\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa4-orchestration\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-transient-contract-tests\\nHEAD 1efb86255b7c0cd685bec1f24b0eb8b72a32e94b\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/pa4-transient-contract-tests\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa4-validation\\nHEAD a4249461206ba30c86dc53a0d0b43a383bede07f\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/rc1\\nHEAD c1fcafa62e669f26c2a289b20e35cf984f53e20b\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-rc1\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v122-rc1-tag\\nHEAD 2d314098d8a5b6d9863d22f791007b27f65d2956\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v122stable\\nHEAD ce2c951e249d581e97dd19bcf1d771de62eb5157\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v122-stable\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-colorize-tui\\nHEAD 073e6ea90b025acbd19f0c1b92ea1c9e93e6937e\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v130-colorize-tui\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-tui-audit-fixes\\nHEAD 93ecfdd2129f753fc0a9a7c11013eaea5eaafa5f\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v130-tui-audit-fixes\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/v130-tui-audit-suite\\nHEAD ca300407effd7ece89ba60d86f57c058f109b5bc\\ndetached\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc6-cargo\\nHEAD 82938c303364edbbb41934bd73ab5a6655e82f2c\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/v12x-vc6-cargo-baseline\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc9-investigation\\nHEAD c674b17683881c7fcd1e553c44932396c0f62fb7\\nbranch refs/heads/v1/vc9-persistence-identity\\n\\nworktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm3-repeat\\nHEAD b0d59908e75a51d57449e17bd348068713f2e567\\ndetached\\n\\na5fa1dca54c176e50f16176fe82454a2dff360e6\\trefs/heads/main\\n{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"jobs\\\":[{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"success\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"cloudflare-experiment (24)\\\",\\\"running\\\":[],\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"test-matrix (24)\\\",\\\"running\\\":[\\\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\\\"],\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"},{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"test-matrix (22)\\\",\\\"running\\\":[\\\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\\\"],\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"},{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"success\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"cloudflare-experiment (22)\\\",\\\"running\\\":[],\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"linux-x86_64\\\",\\\"running\\\":[\\\"Run the CLI suite with a hang bound\\\"],\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}],\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"36999e\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000046808,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":11610,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11610)\\nTotal output lines: 293\\n\\n# Nexus Code v1.3 plan: the platform track\\n\\nStatus: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\\\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\\\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\\n\\n## 1. Purpose and theme\\n\\nFrontier clients are platforms: a CLI plus a desktop app, IDE integration, background and scheduled agents, an SDK or managed API, installable plugin bundles, and enterprise policy (web sources of 2026-11-15, mostly third-party; see [`docs/v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](../v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md) §2). Nexus is a single-binary Linux CLI and TUI. It cannot match breadth or first-party model fit, and a worse copy of three funded platforms is not a goal.\\n\\nThe v1.3 theme: **let other programs drive Nexus and let teams govern it, without weakening the gate.** Every new surface (an IDE over ACP, a scheduler, a hook, a plugin, an SDK caller, a managed policy) goes through the same approval and verification gates as the terminal does. The differentiator to build on is the one nobody else ships by default: a completion you can audit (independent verification, receipts, evidence). Two consequences shape the plan:\\n\\n1. **Nexus as the verification layer for other harnesses.** `nexus verify` and an MCP/ACP `verify` tool let a Codex, Claude Code or Aider session ask Nexus to independently check its work. This costs little (the machinery exists) and is positioned where the platforms are weakest.\\n2. **Governance as a product.** Managed policy with fixed precedence, a hash-chained and optionally signed audit export, and hooks that can only tighten.\\n\\nNon-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\\n\\n## 2. Evidence and its limits\\n\\n- **Nexus today** (read-only inventory, 2026-11-15; \\\"present\\\" means present in source or docs, nothing was run): sidecar/TUI protocol is NDJSON over the sidecar's stdio with `PROTOCOL_VERSION = 1`, an event `v`, a one-way `hello`, typed unions in `cli/src/protocol.ts:53-204` hand-mirrored in `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/protocol.rs`, **no schema file, no socket**; `nexus run --json` returns `TaskExecutionResult` (`cli/src/core/app.ts:44`) with no schema or version, exit codes defined only in code (0 verified, 2 completed-unverified, 130 interrupted, 1 otherwise); `ApplicationCore` is an accidental re-export (`cli/src/index.ts:1240`) and `cli/package.json` has `main` and `bin` but no `exports` or `types`; operations are durable and resumable (per-operation lock, 12 h wait survives restart, `maxConcurrentUnits` capped at 64 and clamped to 1 without `git.isolate`) but **foreground-only**, with no scheduler, cron, trigger or daemon in the code; plugins are plain JS modules with no manifest, checksum or install command, workspace plugins load after `nexus trust` with full process authority, hooks cover pre/post tool only; policy is the workspace-local `.nexus-security.json`, **fails open to defaults on a parse error**, with no system or admin layer; session export is `nexus-session-export/2` reachable only through `/export`; the flight log is append-only by convention (no hash chain found) and the CERI evidence store hashes files but has no signature or verify command; local OTLP-style traces are written on every session with no opt-in gate and no network exporter found; a goal (objective ledger) persists across sessions via `--resume`, but nothing re-launches a task on its own.\\n- **Peers, read from source** (shallow clones of 2026-11-13/14; Codex at commit 336b19a; untrusted code, nothing run): ACP servers in goose (`crates/goose/src/acp`), Cline (`apps/cli/src/acp/acpAgent.ts`), Gemini CLI (`packages/cli/src/acp`) and opencode (`packages/cli/src/acp/agent.ts`), the Codex `app-server` (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, websocket or UDS; schema generated from Rust types and checked in); schedulers in goose (`scheduler/full.rs`), Cline (`*.cron.md` and event specs) and opencode (a `ScheduleTable` with `in`/`at`/`every`/`cron` and an optional deterministic wake `check`); hooks in Gemini CLI (`docs/hooks/reference.md`), goose (`hooks/mod.rs`), Cline and Codex; extension manifests in Gemini CLI (`gemini-extension.json`), goose (Open Plugins and Gemini formats) and opencode; policy tiers in Gemini CLI (`core/src/policy/config.ts:67-71`), opencode (`managed-policy.ts`) and Codex (`requirements.toml`); goal state in opencode (`schema/src/goal.ts`) and Codex (`thread/goal/*`).\\n- **XERJ reference queries** (2026-11-15, local node, read-only; the `opencode` index is flagged \\\"not verified complete\\\", so a miss there is not proof of absence): `xerj code opencode` for ACP, scheduling and plugin manifests; `xerj search --prefix goose` for ACP, scheduler and extension manifests; `xerj search --prefix xc-opencode` for managed policy. **Took:** opencode's wire-level ACP fixture pattern (`packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) for protocol conformance tests; opencode's schedule model (`schema/src/schedule.ts`, `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`: `next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome`, deterministic wake check with hard limits) and its documented GitHub scheduled-workflow example; goose's cron-on-a-recipe scheduler shape (`scheduler/full.rs`, `commands/schedule.rs`); goose's checked-in ACP schema and method metadata (`acp-meta.json`); goose's plugin formats (`plugins/formats/gemini.rs`, `open_plugins.rs`); opencode's managed policy as config statements with an action, a resource and an effect (`config/plugin/policy.ts`). **Rejected:** opencode's in-process Effect plugin hooks (a JS-in-process model is what Nexus is trying to move away from), goose's per-run `Auto` mode for scheduled runs (unattended runs must never get allow-always), and any code copied verbatim: reference code is read-only inspiration.\\n- **Research notes** (2026-11-15): the Anthropic 2026 agentic coding trends report (read in full; a vendor document with no methodology, customer-reported figures) and OpenHands (documentation, not source) support three priorities here: VR-1 and VR-2 (the report's own evidence is that people delegate what is cheaply checkable, so verifiability is the delegation boundary), PA-1 (it names a view of concurrent agent sessions) and AC-1 (above). They are framing, not evidence about Nexus.\\n- **Not covered:** Claude Code and agy were not read (web sources only); parts of Cline's daemon, Codex's hook handler types and plugin manifest, and goose's managed policy were sampled, not read in full; peer behaviour under load was not measured.\\n\\n## 3. Principles\\n\\n1. **Protocol first.** Nothing else in this plan is safe to build until the client protocol is published, schema'd, versioned and conformance-tested.\\n2. **One gate.** A new surface can request an approval but can never answer one on behalf of Nexus's own gates (SI-2, CS-* rules, verification). A client that answers \\\"allow\\\" to a denied action must have no effect; this is tested.\\n3. **Tighten only.** Hooks and managed policy may deny, require a human, or rewrite arguments toward safer values. They never grant.\\n4. **Unattended means stricter.** Scheduled and detached runs never get allow-always, run in a fresh worktree, and record that they were unattended in every receipt.\\n5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\\n6. **Schemas come from types.** Generate JSON Schema from the TypeScript types and check it in with a parity test, so the Rust mirror cannot drift (reference: Codex `app-server-protocol` schema export, goose `acp-schema.json`).\\n7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\\n8. **Experimental is a flag.** New methods sit behind `experimental` at `initialize` (reference: Codex `experimentalApi`) until a conformance suite has run against two real clients.\\n\\n## 4. Workstreams and units\\n\\nSize: S = one PR; M = two to four PRs; L = a program. P0 = prerequisite, P1 = core, P2 = stretch.\\n\\n### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\\n\\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\\n| PF-2 | `nexus run --json` result schema with a `schema_version`, documented exit codes in the manual, `--input-json` or stdin for the task | schema validated in tests; a consumer test parses the output | S | P0 |\\n| PF-3 | Transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener (reference: goose `acp/transport/auth.rs`, constant-time compare); several clients may attach and reattach | a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600 | M | P1 |\\n| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\\n\\n### AC: ACP and IDE access\\n\\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| AC-1 | ACP adapter over the sidecar protocol (stdio), hand-rolled JSON-RPC with no dependency: …6610 tokens truncated…`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\\n\\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\\n\\n| Resource | Rule |\\n|---|---|\\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\\\"$HOME/.config\\\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\\\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\\n| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\\n\\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\\n\\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\\n\\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\\n\\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\\n\\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\\n\\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\\n\\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\\n\\n## 6. Coordination records\\n\\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\\n\\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\\n\\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\\n\\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\\n|---|---|\\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\\n\\n## 8. Independence constraints\\n\\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\\n\"}}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","create_time":1795300490.333334}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":16345,"sampled_through_sequence":16341,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
