{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:59:03.463Z","ordinal":37456,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-896d1163-055b-41e8-8c68-95fff70b6ca4","process_id":"7419","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n \"maxToolInvocations|toolInvocationLimit|TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET\" cli/src"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-tool-invocation-budget","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'maxToolInvocations|toolInvocationLimit|TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET' cli/src","query":"maxToolInvocations|toolInvocationLimit|TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET","path":"cli"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"cli/src/types.ts:76:  maxToolInvocations?: number;\ncli/src/types.ts:166:  maxToolInvocations?: number;\ncli/src/types.ts:199:  if (budget.maxToolInvocations !== undefined && (!Number.isSafeInteger(budget.maxToolInvocations) || budget.maxToolInvocations < 1 || budget.maxToolInvocations > MAX_TOOL_INVOCATIONS)) {\ncli/src/types.ts:209:  maxToolInvocations: DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_INVOCATIONS,\ncli/src/config.ts:435:  const maxToolInvocations = toolInvocationLimit(options);\ncli/src/config.ts:531:    maxToolInvocations,\ncli/src/config.ts:765:    maxToolInvocations: toolInvocationLimit(options),\ncli/src/config.ts:773:export function toolInvocationLimit(options: any): number {\ncli/src/config.ts:774:  const raw = options.maxToolInvocations ?? effectiveSetting(\"NEXUS_MAX_TOOL_INVOCATIONS\");\ncli/src/core/turn.ts:51:  | \"TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\"\ncli/src/core/turn.ts:361:    maxToolInvocations?: number;\ncli/src/core/turn.ts:597:    if (t.reason === \"TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\") {\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:158:  const configuredToolLimit = this.config.maxToolInvocations ?? budget?.maxToolInvocations ?? DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_INVOCATIONS;\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:162:  let maxToolInvocations = configuredToolLimit;\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:164:    const storedLimit = this.objective.continuation.maxToolInvocations;\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:168:    maxToolInvocations = storedLimit === undefined ? configuredToolLimit : Math.min(storedLimit, configuredToolLimit);\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:169:    this.objective.continuation.maxToolInvocations = maxToolInvocations;\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:232:      const admitted = tracker.admitToolInvocations(requested, maxToolInvocations);\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:240:      stopReason = \"TOOL_INVOCATION_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\";\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:242:        ? `${tracker.data.toolInvocations}/${maxToolInvocations}`\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:243:        : `at least ${tracker.data.toolInvocations}/${maxToolInvocations}; tool history before this version is unknown`;\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:916:            : `Not executed: tool invocation budget exhausted (${tracker.data.toolInvocations}/${maxToolInvocations} dispatcher admissions).`);\ncli/src/core/run_turn.ts:1113:              maxToolInvocations,\ncli/src/core/objective.ts:201:  maxToolInvocations?: number;\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":490425878}},"started_at_ms":1795301941971,"completed_at_ms":1795301943462}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:59:03.602Z","ordinal":37457,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-8c7e3fa4-d0e7-4c9f-8f13-af3543100a28","process_id":"3769","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '340,410p' docs/USER_MANUAL.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-tool-invocation-budget","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '340,410p' docs/USER_MANUAL.md","name":"USER_MANUAL.md","path":"docs/USER_MANUAL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"  ```\n  Within one frame, the cancellation signal reaches the command runner and inference engine.\n- **Process Group Termination (REQ-AGT-109, REQ-SEC-106):** Cancellation terminates the entire process group (`killpg`) of in-flight commands: `SIGTERM` is sent immediately, followed by `SIGKILL` after a 2-second grace period. The turn reaches an idle state within 5 seconds.\n- **Repeated Cancel Input:** While cancelling, a second `Esc` has no effect. A second `Ctrl-C` force-quits the TUI and sidecar, initiating an automatic shutdown sweep that terminates all child processes.\n- **Background Services:** Commands launched with `run_command { background: true, service_name }` (such as background dev servers) are registered as managed services. They survive turn cancellation and are listed in `/status`. On client exit or application shutdown, all registered background services are cleaned up. Background processes spawned without registration (`cmd &`) are terminated on turn cancellation.\n- **Inference deadlines:** A model request has a 15-minute whole-request deadline, including streams that keep sending chunks. First-byte and idle-between-chunks timeouts remain separate. The operator may set `LOCAL_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS` up to 60 minutes; zero or an invalid value uses the 15-minute default and cannot disable the deadline. Cancellation aborts the in-flight request.\n- **Notification Gating (REQ-AGT-109a):** Turn completion notifications (desktop `notify-send` and terminal `BEL` `\\x07`) are gated to interactive TTY environments running on the REPL or TUI surface. Headless runs (`nexus run --json`) emit no BEL characters and spawn no notifications.\n\n### Long tasks: bounded automatic continuation\n\n`--max-steps` (default 30) is a segment: at each segment boundary the turn writes a durable checkpoint and continues\non its own. The objective has a finite default ceiling of 500 total steps and each invocation has a 480-minute ceiling.\nOperator overrides are bounded at 5,000 steps and 4,320 minutes. A turn also stops on completion, cancellation, demonstrated lack\nof progress (`--stagnation-steps`, default 12 consecutive steps without progress, or a repeated action on an unchanged\nworkspace after two recovery notes), an actionable failure, or a limit **you** set (`--max-total-steps`,\n`--max-turn-minutes`). Progress is judged against workspace content, not tool-call equality: plan edits, identical\nrewrites and timestamp-only output changes do not count, and a retry after a real change is legitimate. Polls and\ntransient failures are spaced with backoff instead of being treated as loops. `--no-continuation` restores the\nsingle-segment turn. Tracking (cumulative steps, loops, failed approaches, compactions) belongs to the objective and\nsurvives compaction, follow-ups (\"continue\") and `/resume`; the total-step budget is shared with in-turn delegated\nhelpers. The time limit currently applies to each active invocation and is not accumulated across process restarts.\nSee `docs/COMPACTION_AND_CONTINUITY.md`.\n\nA single model response may request at most 16 tool calls. A larger batch is rejected in full before any of its calls\nrun, and Nexus reports the rejection in the next model context. This bounds tool fan-out from one response; it does\nnot replace the objective-wide step budget.\n\nA second, independent brake watches the verifier itself: when `task_completed` fails the same verifier\n`NEXUS_VERIFIER_CONVERGENCE_ATTEMPTS` times in a row (default 3; a check-only failure with no test names needs one\nmore) with the same failing tests across changed workspace states, with the failure alternating between two sets\n(A/B/A/B), or with a rising failing-test count, the turn ends `BLOCKED` / `NOT_ESTABLISHED` with reason\n`VERIFIER_NOT_CONVERGING` and the attempt history attached; you decide how to proceed. Environment failures, timeouts\nand runs skipped on unchanged inputs are ignored, and a changing failure set never trips it. Only `task_completed`\nis covered: a loop of `run_tests` calls alone is bounded by `--stagnation-steps` (`STAGNATION_DETECTED`) instead.\n\nA third brake counts identical tool calls regardless of timing: a call with the same tool name and the same arguments\n(compared as stable JSON) that returns the same result (timings and ids ignored), seen in the last 64 calls even with\nother calls between them. At `NEXUS_REPEATED_CALL_SOFT` repeats (default 3) the model gets a corrective note naming the\ncall. At the hard threshold, in a step that changed nothing in the workspace, the turn ends `BLOCKED` /\n`NOT_ESTABLISHED` with reason `TOOL_CALL_LOOP` and the repeated call and recent call history (arguments redacted)\nattached. The hard threshold depends on the call, with `NEXUS_REPEATED_CALL_HARD` (default 5) as the base:\n\n| Call | Soft | Hard |\n|---|---|---|\n| Workspace reads (`read_file`, `list_dir`, `search_code`, `git_status`, ...): counted only while the workspace is unchanged | 3 | 5 |\n| `run_command`, writes and other tools: a failing rerun between edits with identical output is a loop | 3 | 10 |\n| An unchanged successful external poll (`tail build.log`, `docker ps`), which Nexus already spaces with backoff | 3 | 20 |\n\nA repeat whose result changed, a read after an edit, and a turn whose workspace state cannot be established are never\nstopped (the note still appears). Both settings are whole numbers (soft at least 2, hard above soft); other values are\nignored and `nexus doctor` warns. They work with `--no-continuation`. A project `.env` can only lower them: the\neffective value is the smaller of the project's and your own.\n\nThe operator's objective and its\nnumbered requirements are pinned for the model on every request (never summarised away); the agent records\nrequirement progress with evidence, and a turn that stops with requirements open ends `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE`\nwith the remaining items, failed check and next action (see `/receipt`). Only the operator can reduce scope: a\nrequirement the agent marks descoped stays unresolved until you run `/requirements accept <id>` (`/requirements`\nlists them). While an objective with listed requirements is unfinished, every message you send — however long, even\nwith its own numbered list — continues it (numbered items are added as further requirements, and a notice says so);\n`/requirements clear` (or `/clear`) starts a new objective. For a requirement you wrote, `/requirements reference <id> <command>` names a reference command, and `/requirements differential <id> --inputs <file>` (a JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) runs your latest verifier command and the reference on each input (stdin, sandboxed, no network, 30 s each) and reports how many agree, diverge or were inconclusive (timeout or error). Agreement on those inputs says nothing about other inputs; it is not equivalence. The agent cannot name a reference or run this. A requirement counts as *verified* only when a passing\nverifier run (`run_tests`, or the completion verifier) is recorded on the current workspace; any later change demotes\nit to *implemented (model-reported)*. Each requirement keeps its complete wording; when the objective is too long to\nrepeat on every request, the unresolved requirements are carried in full and the agent reads the rest with\n`read_objective`. Your steering and follow-up messages are carried verbatim the same way, however many and however\nlong, after compaction and after a crash and resume: the newest are repeated in full (about 12,000 characters), and\nany older one that does not fit is named with its size so the agent reads it with `read_objective`. A verifier\nfailure is not re-run while nothing it depends on has changed (same workspace files, no mutating command since, same\ndependency/environment markers, same process).\n\n### Compaction and context sizing\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":610171140}},"started_at_ms":1795301941991,"completed_at_ms":1795301943602}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T22:59:03.731Z","ordinal":37458,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a2003f-4e12-7811-a5cb-72eb7d9f7819","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c1085dae-fbd9-4d77-9130-c4f68aebbe9d","process_id":"34778","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '25,70p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/p10-02-tool-invocation-budget","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '25,70p' docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md","name":"POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md","path":"docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"3. Update ruleset to require review/code-owner approval and strict check freshness. Keep bypass actors empty or explicitly owner-controlled.\n4. Add a canary PR/harness mutation that removes a required check or makes it unconditional and demonstrate merge is blocked by independent policy.\n5. Document emergency bypass ownership and audit record.\n\n**Required tests / acceptance:** API readback confirms required approvals >=1, code-owner approval, strict status checks, no unapproved bypass actor. Seeded workflow change without approval cannot merge. A normal workflow change with independent approval and green required checks can merge. Exact SHA and ruleset JSON archived.\n\n**Migration / rollback:** Existing open PRs may require new approvals and reruns. Rollback is ruleset restoration only by repository owner; retain the last known ruleset export. Residual risk: repository administrators can alter rulesets outside source control.\n\n## WP-P10-02 — Durable task-level execution budgets\n\n**Priority:** P0 · **Complexity:** L · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-01 recommended first; can be implemented independently.\n\n**Gap/evidence:** `DEFAULT_CONTINUATION` sets total steps and minutes to Infinity (`cli/src/types.ts:182-188`); `runTurnImpl` checks a local `currentStep` (`run_turn.ts:137-145,220-267`); `runSubagent` uses an unbounded `for (;;)` (`agent.ts:4440-4457`); resumed invocation recreates local counters while cumulative steps are observational only (`turn.ts:348-359`).\n\n**Affected surfaces:** `types.ts`, `config.ts`, `run_turn.ts`, subagent execution in `agent.ts`, provider recovery, tool deadlines, durable objective/task state, operation runner and CLI/UI reporting.\n\n**Design / reuse:** Extend `ContinuationBudget` and existing durable objective/operation records with a task budget id, maximum total steps/tool invocations/inference submissions/elapsed time/tokens, per-tool deadline and delegation depth. Persist consumed counters before/after side effects with `withDurableSync`. Child agents inherit the parent’s remaining budget; provider retry consumes the same inference allowance. A resumed task cannot regain budget. Require explicit operator-authorized budget renewal after exhaustion.\n\n**Implementation steps:**\n\n1. Define additive schema fields with migration defaults that do not silently claim prior counters are zero; old tasks resume in a conservative bounded mode or require explicit operator choice.\n2. Add a shared budget tracker passed through direct turns, subagents, work units, provider recovery and tool dispatch.\n3. Enforce before dispatch and before any retry; classify exhaustion as `BLOCKED`/`LIMIT_REACHED`, never `VERIFIED` or successful operation completion.\n4. Add ceilings for response streaming and whole-turn wall time, including a finite total timeout for local inference streams; idle timeout remains separate.\n5. Report dimension, configured limit, consumed amount and resume behavior in CLI/TUI result.\n\n**Required tests / acceptance:** Model emits distinct writes indefinitely: run stops at exact step/tool/token/time budget with nonzero/non-success result. Nested subagent shares remaining budget. Repeated provider failures consume retry allowance. Exhaustion followed by process restart and resume does not reset counters. Resume near limit permits only remaining budget. A hung local tool and slow-drip stream stop by configured deadline. Budget persistence write failure prevents new side-effectful dispatch.\n\n**Migration / rollback:** New config defaults may shorten formerly unlimited runs; provide an explicit operator override with a hard documented maximum, not Infinity. Keep old records readable. Rollback must not turn persisted exhausted state into zero; older binaries should refuse unsupported budget schema versions. Residual risk: already-running external calls may finish after cancellation or have unknown outcomes.\n\n## WP-P10-03 — Acceptance evidence integrity and export\n\n**Priority:** P1 · **Complexity:** M · **Dependencies:** WP-P10-01 for independent policy identity.\n\n**Gap/evidence:** Flight events are append-only by convention, but append failures are ignored (`cli/src/flight.ts:77-108`); no chain/signature verification exists. `TurnResult` has `candidateId` and output SHA but not complete environment/policy identity (`cli/src/core/turn.ts:191-219`).\n\n**Affected surfaces:** `flight.ts`, `evidence.ts`, completion oracle, durable state and audit commands.\n\n**Design / reuse:** Add an evidence manifest to existing receipt/event structures. Chain each event over prior digest; include candidate tree/commit identity, verifier definition/config digest, command, tool/runtime versions, exit code, timestamp, actor/model/provider identity and policy version. Use an operator-supplied signing key only if explicitly configured; unsigned bundles must say “integrity checked, not signed.” Required acceptance evidence write failure must produce a visible non-success result; optional telemetry remains best effort and explicitly marked.\n\n**Implementation steps:**\n\n1. Define canonical event encoding and versioned manifest.\n2. Chain existing records and add offline `verify` over an exported bundle; do not duplicate existing state storage.\n3. Bind verification receipt to exact candidate and current policy/workflow identity.\n4. Add redaction before hashing/export and retention controls.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":753518708}},"started_at_ms":1795301941973,"completed_at_ms":1795301943727}}
